When he chose Lyndon Johnson,

00:17:58.570 --> 00:17:59.970
We set into motion a sequence.

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:06.290
of events that left Bobby and LBJ smoldering with resentments that would never go away.

00:18:06.510 --> 00:18:14.620
From the very first, LBJ and Bobby detested each other. It was as Johnson

00:18:15.000 --> 00:18:17.470
He politely described it, a matter of chemistry.

00:18:17.810 --> 00:18:19.520
It was just oil and water.

00:18:20.380 --> 00:18:22.100
Bobby was very moralistic.

00:18:23.020 --> 00:18:25.700
Integrity meant everything to him.

00:18:26.680 --> 00:18:29.480
And LBJ was a politician.

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:30.080
Thank you.

00:18:30.500 --> 00:18:31.300
Robert Kennedy

00:18:32.030 --> 00:18:34.690
spoke the word politician as if it were an obscenity.

00:18:35.590 --> 00:18:37.970
He hated the back slapping, log rolling.

00:18:38.570 --> 00:18:40.630
all of the horse-trading of politics.

00:18:41.290 --> 00:18:44.250
The sort of thing that Lyndon Johnson absolutely loved.

00:18:45.290 --> 00:18:47.110
Bobby dislikes Johnson intensely.

00:18:47.980 --> 00:18:48.400
But...

00:18:48.650 --> 00:18:50.110
Jack Kennedy is calling the shots.

00:18:50.730 --> 00:18:55.690
And what Jack Kennedy understands is that as a northeastern Irish Catholic,

00:18:56.230 --> 00:18:59.330
He needs very much to get Southern votes.

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:04.240
it becomes essential to bring someone like Lyndon Johnson onto the ticket.

00:19:04.920 --> 00:19:08.100
But once they let out the word that they're going to make

00:19:08.100 --> 00:19:10.900
Lyndon Johnson the vice presidential nominee...

00:19:11.390 --> 00:19:12.670
labor and

00:19:13.280 --> 00:19:14.380
liberals in the party.

00:19:14.710 --> 00:19:15.290
throw a fit.

00:19:15.580 --> 00:19:21.160
Bobby is charged by his brother with the responsibility of talking Johnson off the ticket.

00:19:23.960 --> 00:19:26.260
Bobby confronted Johnson in his hotel room.

00:19:27.100 --> 00:19:29.980
and told him flatly that JFK no longer wants

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:32.080
wanted him to be the vice presidential nominee.

00:19:33.830 --> 00:19:34.470
LBJ.

00:19:35.020 --> 00:19:36.000
didn't believe him.

00:19:37.060 --> 00:19:37.780
Insulted.

00:19:38.250 --> 00:19:39.330
He refused to quit.

00:19:40.040 --> 00:19:41.560
Johnson wants to be vice president.

00:19:41.900 --> 00:19:42.860
He's not going to back down.

00:19:43.210 --> 00:19:44.130
He's not going to give up.

00:19:44.460 --> 00:19:45.560
And he thinks

00:19:45.960 --> 00:19:49.420
that Bobby is operating without Jack's approval.

00:19:51.140 --> 00:19:53.900
Bobby's been out of touch, Jack leaked to a reporter.

00:19:55.180 --> 00:19:56.380
He doesn't know what's happening.

00:19:57.580 --> 00:19:59.980
Bobby is absolutely taking the fall for his brother here.

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:02.460
and that suits John Kennedy perfectly well.

00:20:02.860 --> 00:20:05.640
And Johnson has fallen for this head fake,

00:20:06.020 --> 00:20:07.860
but it is absolutely inconceivable

00:20:07.860 --> 00:20:09.580
to anybody who knew the Kennedy brothers

00:20:09.580 --> 00:20:12.180
that Robert Kennedy would be acting on his own

00:20:12.180 --> 00:20:15.470
to split up his brother's presidential ticket

00:20:15.470 --> 00:20:17.750
within hours of the deal being sealed.

00:20:17.950 --> 00:20:19.370
And I am grateful, finally,

00:20:20.510 --> 00:20:23.800
that I can rely in the coming months on many others,

00:20:24.960 --> 00:20:26.440
on a distinguished running mate

00:20:26.440 --> 00:20:28.900
who brings unity and strength.

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:32.420
To our platform and our ticket, Lyndon Johnson.

00:20:35.580 --> 00:20:38.880
Lyndon Johnson will never forgive Robert Kennedy for this.

00:20:39.500 --> 00:20:43.020
The manner of the selection of Lyndon Johnson as Vice President

00:20:43.950 --> 00:20:46.510
severed the relationship between the two of them permanently,

00:20:46.530 --> 00:20:47.950
and there would be no turning back.

00:20:48.860 --> 00:20:51.240
For the rest of his life, Johnson will say that

00:20:51.240 --> 00:20:53.260
John Kennedy offered me the Vice Presidency,

00:20:53.520 --> 00:20:56.100
and in the dark of night, Bobby Kennedy came downstairs

00:20:56.100 --> 00:20:57.780
to try to take it away from me.

00:21:00.730 --> 00:21:03.930
The election of 1916 was too close to call.

00:21:06.850 --> 00:21:10.110
JFK won by only 120,000 votes.

00:21:10.580 --> 00:21:13.850
As one Kennedy aide described it, a gnat's eyebrow.

00:21:16.590 --> 00:21:18.530
Bobby had driven himself relentlessly,

00:21:18.940 --> 00:21:21.120
and JFK was forever grateful.

00:21:22.500 --> 00:21:23.520
He's the hardest worker.

00:21:23.800 --> 00:21:27.200
He's the greatest organizer, President John Kennedy said.

00:21:27.940 --> 00:21:29.960
Easily the best man I've ever...

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:39.070
As JFK prepared to assume the reins of power,

00:21:39.670 --> 00:21:41.390
he wanted Bobby by his side

00:21:41.390 --> 00:21:45.130
and appointed his 35-year-old brother attorney general.

00:21:45.620 --> 00:21:47.000
It was a controversial appointment.

00:21:47.580 --> 00:21:50.120
He had never been a lawyer in a courtroom.

00:21:51.280 --> 00:21:53.200
And it was nepotism.

00:21:53.600 --> 00:21:55.300
I mean, he was the brother of the president.

00:21:56.040 --> 00:21:57.460
His experience was zero.

00:21:57.810 --> 00:21:59.980
He'd been a lawyer for...

00:22:00.320 --> 00:22:06.160
senate committees a zealot with no understanding of the terrible responsibilities of an attorney

00:22:06.160 --> 00:22:19.060
general i was appalled i thought it was a simply awful idea obby surprised everyone moving quickly

00:22:19.060 --> 00:22:23.780
to show he was more than just the president's brother there are a number of different areas

00:22:23.780 --> 00:22:29.980
and where action is needed i think that in the field of organized crime i think it's in the very

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:32.720
the serious situation that's facing the country at the present time.

00:22:33.430 --> 00:22:35.410
Within two weeks of taking office,

00:22:35.850 --> 00:22:39.970
he became the first attorney general ever to declare war on crime.

00:22:42.340 --> 00:22:44.500
He proved a hard, tireless worker,

00:22:44.580 --> 00:22:47.000
and his casual, freewheeling style

00:22:47.000 --> 00:22:51.070
was a striking departure from the formality of bureaucratic Washington.

00:22:52.840 --> 00:22:55.960
He was unlike any other attorney general I've known.

00:22:57.480 --> 00:22:59.460
He wandered around in his shirt sleeves.

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:02.560
He had his big, huge dog, Broomis, in his office.

00:23:03.650 --> 00:23:07.270
The kids came in, the children's drawings were on the wall.

00:23:08.860 --> 00:23:12.860
If you saw Bob Kennedy in a tie at his office, it would be way down open.

00:23:13.700 --> 00:23:20.440
His office was a place of constant motion and doing things unexpected, breaking the schedule.

00:23:21.660 --> 00:23:23.940
He was not a sit-behind-your-desk man.

00:23:25.450 --> 00:23:29.970
But just three months after Bobby had started his new job, he received an...

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:31.860
urgent summons to the Oval Office.

00:23:32.920 --> 00:23:35.920
The president was face to face with disaster.

00:23:40.260 --> 00:23:44.140
JFK had gone along with a CIA plan to invade Cuba

00:23:44.140 --> 00:23:46.460
with a small army of Cuban exiles.

00:23:46.760 --> 00:23:50.810
The president had been assured that the Cuban people

00:23:50.810 --> 00:23:52.970
would rise up against the communist government

00:23:52.970 --> 00:23:58.760
of Fidel Castro and revolt, but the CIA had been wrong.

00:24:01.530 --> 00:24:05.130
Bobby watched helplessly as his brother wrestled with catastrophe.

00:24:06.290 --> 00:24:10.640
The invaders were under attack. Castro's army was taking prisoners.

00:24:12.090 --> 00:24:20.240
They can't do this to you, Bobby said. But the invasion failed. JFK had fumbled badly.

00:24:23.310 --> 00:24:28.630
The president would turn to his brother for advice and counsel, as he never had before.

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:32.800
Traditionally, attorneys general have nothing to do with foreign policy.

00:24:33.000 --> 00:24:37.080
But after the Bay of Pigs, John Kennedy really wanted Bobby by his side,

00:24:37.200 --> 00:24:39.080
helping him to make the decisions on everything,

00:24:39.450 --> 00:24:41.590
because there was only one person in the world

00:24:41.590 --> 00:24:45.010
that John Kennedy trusted unequivocally, and that was Robert Kennedy.

00:24:45.250 --> 00:24:49.150
I think that brought them much closer together than they had been earlier.

00:24:49.780 --> 00:24:54.830
There was, I think, a recognition for the first time by Jack

00:24:54.830 --> 00:24:58.510
on a need for Bobby rather than simply a use of Bobby.

00:24:59.170 --> 00:24:59.910
You would think

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:05.460
that after the Bay of Pigs, the lesson would be don't mess with Castro. Bobby's nature, however,

00:25:05.780 --> 00:25:09.400
was not when the yellow light was blinking to hit the brakes, but rather to hit the gas.

00:25:11.730 --> 00:25:19.430
Castro became Bobby's obsession. Working closely with the CIA, Bobby launched a secret war against

00:25:19.430 --> 00:25:25.580
him, codenamed Operation Mongoose. All kinds of things are tried, all kinds.

00:25:26.500 --> 00:25:29.510
Try to infiltrate the military, have a coup or revolt.

00:25:30.190 --> 00:25:38.070
sabotage. He was pushing, always pushing. Bobby Kennedy wanted things blown up, so we blew things

00:25:38.070 --> 00:25:48.450
up. Nothing was off limits. Between 1960 and 1965, the CIA made at least eight separate attempts

00:25:48.450 --> 00:25:55.820
on Fidel Castro's life. Neither of the Kennedys was ever directly linked to any of them. The

00:25:55.820 --> 00:25:59.960
orders I got were get rid of Castro, quote unquote, and I kept that.

00:26:00.000 --> 00:26:01.680
asking him, what do you mean by get rid of?

00:26:02.230 --> 00:26:03.650
Can you be a little bit more specific?

00:26:04.090 --> 00:26:05.490
What do you want to happen?

00:26:05.610 --> 00:26:07.710
What do you want to see as an end result?

00:26:08.720 --> 00:26:10.300
They said, we want him to disappear.

00:26:11.540 --> 00:26:13.480
And it's left up to you as a senior officer

00:26:13.480 --> 00:26:16.400
to decide what your limits are, if any.

00:26:17.920 --> 00:26:22.950
My own conclusion is that Bobby never quite came out

00:26:22.950 --> 00:26:25.210
and said, you must kill this guy.

00:26:25.940 --> 00:26:27.280
You will never find good evidence.

00:26:27.440 --> 00:26:28.480
They didn't write it down.

00:26:30.220 --> 00:26:34.360
Joe Kennedy once said to Bobby, never write it down.

00:26:34.890 --> 00:26:36.610
Old Irish rule in Boston.

00:26:38.390 --> 00:26:41.670
Bobby attempted to oust Castro for nearly two years,

00:26:41.690 --> 00:26:45.990
but his efforts only seemed to stiffen Cuban resolve.

00:26:47.790 --> 00:26:48.770
Total waste of effort.

00:26:49.300 --> 00:26:50.860
I tried to tell him that at the time.

00:26:51.460 --> 00:26:52.720
We were not going to succeed.

00:26:53.370 --> 00:26:55.730
Fidel was going to be there, and he's going to stay there.

00:26:57.090 --> 00:26:59.970
But Bobby Kennedy didn't understand what his...

00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:05.060
limits were. No matter what we told him, no matter what we tried to do, no matter how

00:27:05.060 --> 00:27:08.500
many people we put on the job, he was always screaming for murder.

00:27:30.030 --> 00:27:35.020
In 1961, Bobby Kennedy was 36 years old and the father of seven children.

00:27:36.840 --> 00:27:42.420
After a long day at the Justice Department, he returned home to a Civil War mansion just

00:27:42.420 --> 00:27:45.100
outside of Washington called Hickory Hill.

00:27:49.350 --> 00:27:52.690
It was a household where there was constantly something going on.

00:27:53.830 --> 00:27:57.290
Bob was overloaded at work, but he always took time for those children.

00:28:02.490 --> 00:28:04.110
There was a lot of physical affection.

00:28:05.540 --> 00:28:07.300
Saturday or Sunday, all the children

00:28:07.300 --> 00:28:10.700
would pile into my parents' beds and tickle each other.

00:28:10.820 --> 00:28:12.160
And it was called a tickle tumble.

00:28:13.870 --> 00:28:15.490
Well, of course, there were plenty of dogs,

00:28:16.250 --> 00:28:17.190
plenty of horses.

00:28:17.410 --> 00:28:20.850
My brother Bobby collected, you know, reptiles.

00:28:21.090 --> 00:28:23.820
We had a cotamundi, hawks, falcons.

00:28:24.740 --> 00:28:26.520
It was a menagerie.

00:28:34.540 --> 00:28:37.640
Hickory Hill was the center of Bobby's private world.

00:28:41.400 --> 00:28:43.560
To be invited to a Hickory Hill party

00:28:43.750 --> 00:28:48.470
signaled acceptance into an exclusive community of power and privilege.

00:28:49.290 --> 00:28:53.370
Hickory Hill party was a weird mixture of kind of fraternity party

00:28:53.370 --> 00:28:55.870
and high-minded salon.

00:28:56.470 --> 00:28:59.980
And it was kind of a fun mix of both because...

00:29:00.000 --> 00:29:04.280
did talk about serious issues. They had Hickory Hill seminars where they would bring in famous

00:29:04.280 --> 00:29:10.040
people to lecture about child development or juvenile crime or the future of the Cold

00:29:10.040 --> 00:29:16.020
War. At the same time, they're goofing around and they're playing charades. It was an odd

00:29:16.020 --> 00:29:23.680
mixture of high sophistication and childish hijinks. There was a childlike quality to Bobby

00:29:23.680 --> 00:29:29.300
Kennedy. Bobby was always asking what flavor ice cream you like.

00:29:30.000 --> 00:29:41.830
was chocolate there were always plenty of games including bobby's favorite touch football i really

00:29:41.830 --> 00:29:48.840
didn't like to play the kennedy branded touch football the rules were crazy they made up the

00:29:48.840 --> 00:29:55.210
rules as they went along or they had their own rules i played a couple of times but i'd usually

00:29:55.210 --> 00:29:59.900
try to find something to do you know somebody had to make a phone call or i have to go

00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:06.660
bathroom they were so competitive with each other it was no real fun to play

00:30:06.660 --> 00:30:17.590
when all the guests had gone home Bobby and Ethel assembled their children for an

00:30:17.590 --> 00:30:26.970
evening ritual my mother and my father shared a belief that we are on earth for

00:30:26.970 --> 00:30:29.650
a short period of time and that we are actually

00:30:30.350 --> 00:30:36.790
children of god we prayed every night that john kennedy would be the best president ever and that

00:30:36.790 --> 00:30:48.980
my father would be the best attorney general ever the kennedy brothers were now a team jack needed his

00:30:48.980 --> 00:30:53.610
brother and nowhere more than with a problem that had begun to tear the country apart

00:30:53.610 --> 00:30:57.450
the struggle of african americans for equal rights

00:31:02.010 --> 00:31:09.830
In the spring of 1961, a group called the Freedom Riders set out to integrate bus stations across the South.

00:31:10.010 --> 00:31:22.600
In a little Alabama town called Anniston, an angry mob attacked them, burning their bus, beating them mercilessly.

00:31:30.220 --> 00:31:33.370
The Freedom Rides was very dangerous.

00:31:35.170 --> 00:31:37.550
Just our very being, our very presence,

00:31:38.480 --> 00:31:39.880
was very, very dangerous.

00:31:42.470 --> 00:31:45.550
During those days, it was impossible for a person of color

00:31:45.700 --> 00:31:48.000
to get on a bus in the South

00:31:48.690 --> 00:31:51.070
without being forced to go to the back of the bus

00:31:51.070 --> 00:31:54.150
or go to a waiting room marked Colored Waiting,

00:31:55.900 --> 00:31:59.600
or use restroom facility marked Colored Men.

00:32:00.280 --> 00:32:05.160
colored women. We wanted to brain down those signs.

00:32:07.800 --> 00:32:12.810
The Freedom Riders were acting on their constitutional rights. Federal statute

00:32:12.810 --> 00:32:18.390
outlawed segregation in bus terminals used in interstate transportation. And as Attorney

00:32:18.390 --> 00:32:22.420
General, Robert Kennedy was obligated to enforce the law.

00:32:23.080 --> 00:32:28.890
I didn't know a great deal about Robert Kennedy. I knew he was a brother.

00:32:30.090 --> 00:32:31.630
of the president of the United States.

00:32:32.520 --> 00:32:33.840
I knew he was the attorney general.

00:32:35.500 --> 00:32:38.560
I wanted him to intervene.

00:32:39.390 --> 00:32:40.790
But Bobby was reluctant.

00:32:41.880 --> 00:32:44.360
The rage and resistance from white Southerners

00:32:44.360 --> 00:32:46.290
had taken him by surprise.

00:32:47.340 --> 00:32:49.760
Before I became attorney general, he admitted,

00:32:50.550 --> 00:32:54.250
I won't say I lay awake at night worrying about civil rights.

00:32:54.930 --> 00:32:57.070
I think he was slow and late

00:32:57.070 --> 00:32:59.930
in getting it about the civil rights world.

00:33:00.000 --> 00:33:05.180
Robert Kennedy is saying what most of the establishment said in that period,

00:33:05.420 --> 00:33:07.700
it's a good idea, but it's the wrong time.

00:33:08.780 --> 00:33:11.740
I don't think any of us going into the department,

00:33:12.020 --> 00:33:13.840
despite our views on civil rights,

00:33:14.180 --> 00:33:17.560
really appreciated how mean it was in the South

00:33:17.560 --> 00:33:19.620
and how dangerous it was.

00:33:20.430 --> 00:33:23.270
Bobby used to compare the discrimination against blacks

00:33:23.270 --> 00:33:25.960
to the no Irish wanted.

00:33:26.220 --> 00:33:29.520
And, you know, that was a wrong comparison.

00:33:30.410 --> 00:33:34.730
One I think was actually resented by blacks, despite the fact that he meant it well.

00:33:35.480 --> 00:33:38.880
They didn't know black people. They didn't know black pain.

00:33:39.660 --> 00:33:41.580
They were not comfortable with black people.

00:33:41.880 --> 00:33:53.780
So there was no reason to expect, I can say in retrospect, them to be wise and passionate about this.

00:33:54.450 --> 00:33:59.970
The president and the attorney general both realized that the civil rights issue...

00:34:00.000 --> 00:34:03.100
was the hot rail in American politics.

00:34:03.860 --> 00:34:06.880
They had won an election by 100,000 votes.

00:34:07.060 --> 00:34:11.560
They didn't really command a working majority in Congress.

00:34:11.560 --> 00:34:13.780
It was very narrow on any issue.

00:34:13.980 --> 00:34:16.080
The Southern Democrats defecting

00:34:16.080 --> 00:34:20.800
would have meant the loss of any legislative agenda.

00:34:23.460 --> 00:34:26.180
As the Freedom Riders traveled deeper into Alabama,

00:34:27.090 --> 00:34:29.970
Bobby, fearing more violence, was determined...

00:34:30.000 --> 00:34:30.860
to stop them.

00:34:33.210 --> 00:34:36.030
Tell them, he told the special deputy for civil rights,

00:34:36.440 --> 00:34:37.340
to call it off.

00:34:37.480 --> 00:34:40.500
He got on the phone and said,

00:34:40.780 --> 00:34:42.320
stop those freedom riders.

00:34:43.380 --> 00:34:45.820
The president's about to go to Vienna to meet Khrushchev.

00:34:45.980 --> 00:34:47.680
It's embarrassing us before the world.

00:34:47.860 --> 00:34:48.240
Stop it.

00:34:49.540 --> 00:34:50.420
They were underway.

00:34:51.720 --> 00:34:53.280
They were not a stoppable group.

00:34:54.600 --> 00:34:57.830
Black people in the South had gotten a sense

00:34:57.830 --> 00:34:59.970
of their efficacy as people.

00:35:00.060 --> 00:35:05.680
and as citizens, and to have Bob Kennedy's Justice Department

00:35:05.680 --> 00:35:08.990
tell them what to do would have been taking a major step backward.

00:35:10.250 --> 00:35:11.150
Who are you going to listen to?

00:35:11.250 --> 00:35:12.630
Are you going to listen to the black people of the South,

00:35:12.750 --> 00:35:14.770
or are you going to listen to the kid from Massachusetts?

00:35:14.910 --> 00:35:15.950
Well, that's an easy answer.

00:35:17.570 --> 00:35:21.130
Bob was angry that they were upsetting everything by doing this.

00:35:21.600 --> 00:35:23.440
He wanted the civil rights movement

00:35:23.440 --> 00:35:25.280
to focus on winning the right to vote,

00:35:25.420 --> 00:35:29.060
and he didn't like his more stately agenda being upset.

00:35:29.680 --> 00:35:29.980
But...

00:35:30.000 --> 00:35:33.130
Once it was upset, then Bobby went into action.

00:35:33.910 --> 00:35:38.040
Desperate to find a way to protect the riders, frantically improvising,

00:35:38.460 --> 00:35:43.500
Bobby sent his aide, John Siegenthaler, to meet with Alabama Governor John Patterson.

00:35:44.150 --> 00:35:47.450
I go in and sit down and I say, I'm from the Attorney General.

00:35:47.970 --> 00:35:51.040
These people have to be given safe conduct.

00:35:52.290 --> 00:35:54.720
He says, we can't protect them.

00:35:55.260 --> 00:35:57.100
I am telling you, it is impossible.

00:35:57.980 --> 00:35:59.980
All the people we've got in this state upset about...

00:36:00.000 --> 00:36:02.060
I mean, of course people are violent.

00:36:02.280 --> 00:36:04.160
These people are coming in asking for a fight,

00:36:04.180 --> 00:36:05.220
and they're going to get a fight.

00:36:06.600 --> 00:36:09.680
That evening, we stayed in the waiting room

00:36:09.680 --> 00:36:12.920
at the Greyhound bus station in Birmingham all night.

00:36:13.890 --> 00:36:15.830
We kept hearing rumors that Robert Kennedy

00:36:15.830 --> 00:36:19.170
was trying to negotiate a way for us to leave Birmingham.

00:36:19.750 --> 00:36:22.290
He kept saying to people we had a right to travel.

00:36:22.580 --> 00:36:27.190
I said, Governor, look, if you can't protect them,

00:36:27.750 --> 00:36:29.610
we don't have any choice.

00:36:30.060 --> 00:36:34.020
Either we've got to have marshals come in and protect them or troops come in and protect them.

00:36:34.080 --> 00:36:35.900
That's the last thing you want, the last thing.

00:36:36.000 --> 00:36:36.980
He banged the table.

00:36:37.220 --> 00:36:42.600
He said, if marshals or troops come into Alabama, blood will run in the streets.

00:36:46.020 --> 00:36:47.940
Siegenthaler and Patterson struck a deal.

00:36:48.660 --> 00:36:50.880
The Freedom Riders would have state protection.

00:36:53.140 --> 00:36:55.920
As they headed toward the Alabama capital in Montgomery,

00:36:56.650 --> 00:36:59.530
Bobby believed that they would be given safe passage.

00:37:02.590 --> 00:37:03.550
It was so strange.

00:37:03.750 --> 00:37:06.570
It was eerie, frightening.

00:37:06.790 --> 00:37:12.660
It was so quiet, so peaceful.

00:37:15.190 --> 00:37:17.410
And the moment we arrived at the bus station,

00:37:18.360 --> 00:37:21.800
the very moment we started down the steps off of that bus,

00:37:22.760 --> 00:37:24.520
a mob came out of nowhere.

00:37:25.420 --> 00:37:27.480
And they beat and beat the reporters,

00:37:27.760 --> 00:37:29.000
and you saw blood everywhere.

00:37:30.600 --> 00:37:32.060
and then they turned on us.

00:37:34.350 --> 00:37:37.180
I was sitting ahead by a member of the mob

00:37:37.180 --> 00:37:43.460
with a wooden crate, and my seatmate was beaten so bad,

00:37:43.640 --> 00:37:46.770
I found myself laying in a pool of blood.

00:37:50.010 --> 00:37:51.610
As I pulled up to the bus station,

00:37:52.500 --> 00:37:53.500
you could hear the screams.

00:37:54.920 --> 00:37:56.340
I just leaped out of the car,

00:37:57.180 --> 00:37:59.760
and at that moment, they wheeled me around.

00:38:00.060 --> 00:38:08.990
said two guys and said what do you think you're doing i said magic words get back i'm with a

00:38:08.990 --> 00:38:17.980
federal government and as i turned back they hit me with a pipe right here robert kennedy became

00:38:17.980 --> 00:38:24.800
educated in a real hurry and i tell you a thing that stilled it for him perhaps more than anything

00:38:24.800 --> 00:38:28.770
Yes, after John Singlethala was beaten.

00:38:30.000 --> 00:38:30.940
someone that he knew.

00:38:31.530 --> 00:38:35.140
I think everything he thought the administration of justice

00:38:35.140 --> 00:38:37.340
and law enforcement was supposed to be about

00:38:37.900 --> 00:38:40.560
had been violated, that it was an outrage,

00:38:40.800 --> 00:38:42.720
that it was a stain on law enforcement

00:38:43.140 --> 00:38:44.340
to let that happen.

00:38:47.800 --> 00:38:49.540
Frustrated by Southern officials,

00:38:50.250 --> 00:38:52.350
Bobby ordered the Interstate Commerce Commission

00:38:52.350 --> 00:38:56.190
to enforce the ban on segregation in public transportation.

00:38:59.030 --> 00:38:59.970
All of those signs...

00:39:00.000 --> 00:39:07.120
that said white waiting, colored waiting, white men, colored men, white women, colored women.

00:39:07.340 --> 00:39:09.340
Those signs came toning down.

00:39:12.100 --> 00:39:15.140
He began to understand civil rights in stages.

00:39:16.690 --> 00:39:25.620
Very few people who are in great positions of power actually go through real interior change.

00:39:26.560 --> 00:39:29.960
But Robert Kennedy really did change, and that changed.

00:39:30.000 --> 00:39:33.140
I think, began around civil rights.

00:39:41.660 --> 00:39:44.820
While civil rights protests and demonstrations continued,

00:39:45.400 --> 00:39:49.120
the Kennedy administration was faced with yet another crisis.

00:39:52.790 --> 00:39:56.510
On October 16, 1962, the president learned

00:39:56.510 --> 00:39:59.900
that the Soviet Union was deploying nuclear missiles.

00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:00.540
in Cuba.

00:40:01.050 --> 00:40:04.810
capable of vaporizing American cities along the East Coast.

00:40:05.240 --> 00:40:06.040
within minutes.

00:40:07.350 --> 00:40:11.210
is the most dangerous moment in all human history.

00:40:12.120 --> 00:40:19.240
never before are two contending powers possessed between them the technical capacity to blow up the world.

00:40:20.860 --> 00:40:24.220
As the president's advisers debated the American response,

00:40:24.540 --> 00:40:27.020
Bobby's first instinct was to lash out.

00:40:27.350 --> 00:40:29.670
pressing for airstrikes to destroy the bases.

00:40:30.280 --> 00:40:32.620
even for an invasion of the island itself.

00:40:33.830 --> 00:40:40.610
On the first day, when they discover the missiles, all Bobby wants to do is stage a provocation and bomb Cuba.

00:40:40.810 --> 00:40:42.730
I mean, he's a complete and total hawk.

00:40:43.340 --> 00:40:44.820
But this is the significant part.

00:40:45.320 --> 00:40:46.440
He changes his mind.

00:40:47.620 --> 00:40:49.980
after hours and hours of intense debate.

00:40:50.570 --> 00:40:52.090
Bobby began to reconsider.

00:40:53.450 --> 00:40:57.970
We've talked for 15 years about the Russians making the first strike against us, he said.

00:40:58.410 --> 00:40:59.570
And we'd never do that.

00:41:00.220 --> 00:41:00.640
Now.

00:41:01.020 --> 00:41:02.780
to do that to a small country.

00:41:03.740 --> 00:41:05.340
It's a hell of a burden to carry.

00:41:06.170 --> 00:41:09.470
It's Robert Kennedy who makes the powerful moral argument

00:41:09.470 --> 00:41:13.330
that to invade Cuba would be to wage a Pearl Harbor in reverse,

00:41:13.690 --> 00:41:15.650
that America simply didn't do this.

00:41:15.770 --> 00:41:17.850
We simply didn't wage preemptive attacks.

00:41:18.030 --> 00:41:19.610
We didn't strike without warning.

00:41:19.930 --> 00:41:26.710
This argument didn't carry the day, but it was an important argument and it started to affect and slow down this rush to war.

00:41:27.640 --> 00:41:29.980
I was very much surprised by Bobby's performance.

00:41:30.000 --> 00:41:30.300
instruments.

00:41:30.930 --> 00:41:32.770
Undersecretary of State George Ball said.

00:41:33.380 --> 00:41:37.360
I always had a feeling that Bobby had a much too simplistic position toward things.

00:41:38.060 --> 00:41:41.080
But he behaved quite differently during the Cuban Missile Crisis.

00:41:41.800 --> 00:41:42.780
Thank you.

00:41:46.120 --> 00:41:48.120
The crisis lasted 13 days.

00:41:48.810 --> 00:41:51.590
until the Russians finally agreed to withdraw the missiles.

00:41:52.010 --> 00:41:55.510
in exchange for a public American pledge not to invade Cuba.

00:41:56.420 --> 00:41:57.520
and his secret promise,

00:41:57.900 --> 00:41:59.960
to withdraw American missiles from Turkey.

00:42:00.000 --> 00:42:00.980
We'll be right back.

00:42:03.540 --> 00:42:04.920
Bobby had hardly slept.

00:42:05.780 --> 00:42:06.840
Rarely gone home.

00:42:09.240 --> 00:42:12.000
An aide who walked into his office remarked,

00:42:12.470 --> 00:42:13.790
Something is different in here.

00:42:15.060 --> 00:42:15.980
Bobby replied,

00:42:17.240 --> 00:42:17.900
I'm older.

00:42:30.020 --> 00:42:30.940
hurting us most.

00:42:31.210 --> 00:42:34.170
is the continued existence of segregation and discrimination.

00:42:34.820 --> 00:42:37.180
And we think we are rendering a great service.

00:42:37.540 --> 00:42:38.240
to our nation.

00:42:38.470 --> 00:42:40.970
For this is not a struggle for ourselves alone.

00:42:41.410 --> 00:42:43.750
It is a struggle to save the soul of America.

00:42:45.060 --> 00:42:47.560
While the Kennedys were confronting the Soviet Union,

00:42:48.100 --> 00:42:50.680
the civil rights movement had not gone away.

00:42:52.040 --> 00:42:53.540
Bobby was determined to help.

00:42:54.280 --> 00:42:55.600
but on his own terms.

00:42:57.190 --> 00:42:59.750
He was growing increasingly exasperated.

00:43:00.180 --> 00:43:05.000
with a man who was emerging as the movement's most conspicuous and inspiring leader.

00:43:05.750 --> 00:43:06.910
Martin Luther King.

00:43:07.860 --> 00:43:10.620
The tension between King and Bob Kennedy was inevitable.

00:43:11.610 --> 00:43:13.830
Here was an uncontrollable force.

00:43:14.140 --> 00:43:15.040
Moral force

00:43:15.480 --> 00:43:18.960
a person who was as much his own man.

00:43:19.550 --> 00:43:21.010
as Robert Kennedy was.

00:43:22.490 --> 00:43:23.390
But I'd like to...

00:43:23.740 --> 00:43:27.260
you know, control his agenda, and King's business was to, you know.

00:43:27.800 --> 00:43:29.520
overthrow people's agendas.

00:43:30.630 --> 00:43:32.350
King, I think, worried about Bobby.

00:43:32.610 --> 00:43:36.230
He worried that he wasn't morally committed enough.

00:43:36.650 --> 00:43:38.690
He didn't sense the passion in Bobby.

00:43:39.300 --> 00:43:45.340
On the other hand, he had great hope that Bobby's readiness to use power would be turned to civil rights.

00:43:45.830 --> 00:43:46.470
He had hope.

00:43:46.760 --> 00:43:47.360
But he worried.

00:43:50.600 --> 00:43:52.440
On May 3, 1963,

00:43:53.260 --> 00:43:56.620
King sent non-violent protesters into the streets of Birmingham.

00:43:57.350 --> 00:43:59.670
to boycott the city's segregated businesses.

00:44:03.960 --> 00:44:07.640
As the Birmingham police used high-pressure hoses to disperse them,

00:44:08.460 --> 00:44:10.140
Bobby watched it all on television.

00:44:15.180 --> 00:44:17.230
Many of the protesters were children.

00:44:22.840 --> 00:44:26.600
He was furious, caught between a civil rights movement

00:44:26.600 --> 00:44:29.970
that refused to back down, and the violent antagonist

00:44:30.000 --> 00:44:38.510
Antagonism of the South that a police chief would set police dogs and fire hoses on people for demanding to be treated

00:44:38.510 --> 00:44:47.550
Fairly in a department store that was bound to hit at his moral core the events entirely

00:44:47.550 --> 00:44:56.800
Transformed Robert Kennedy he started out thinking that it would be better if people would calm down slow down and let gradual improvement

00:44:56.800 --> 00:44:57.740
Take place

00:45:00.000 --> 00:45:05.520
Bobby learned that you couldn't wait and that black people were entitled to the most elementary

00:45:05.520 --> 00:45:06.560
rights now.

00:45:08.090 --> 00:45:13.250
That summer, Bobby urged his brother to put a civil rights bill before Congress that would

00:45:13.250 --> 00:45:15.950
put an end to segregation and public accommodations.

00:45:16.090 --> 00:45:19.730
But the president and his advisers hesitated.

00:45:20.420 --> 00:45:24.640
If you put civil rights legislation before Congress, it was going to tie up the Congress

00:45:24.640 --> 00:45:26.180
for the best part of the next year.

00:45:26.360 --> 00:45:29.800
And that meant that whatever other programs you had were going to be.

00:45:30.160 --> 00:45:35.630
put on a back burner, but Bobby had convinced his brother that not only was it right, but

00:45:35.630 --> 00:45:41.160
for the first time it was possible, and that the president really has to take leadership

00:45:41.210 --> 00:45:42.690
on a moral issue.

00:45:43.960 --> 00:45:46.480
We are confronted primarily with a moral issue.

00:45:47.280 --> 00:45:51.620
It is as old as the scriptures and is as clear as the American Constitution.

00:45:52.850 --> 00:45:59.170
The heart of the question is whether all Americans are to be afforded equal rights and equal

00:45:59.170 --> 00:45:59.550
opportunities.

00:46:01.150 --> 00:46:03.230
whether we are going to treat our fellow Americans

00:46:03.230 --> 00:46:04.690
as we want to be treated.

00:46:05.640 --> 00:46:08.380
It was probably the first time in the history of our country

00:46:08.380 --> 00:46:11.790
that an American president was set

00:46:11.790 --> 00:46:15.310
at the question of civil rights, the question of race.

00:46:16.410 --> 00:46:17.530
It was a moral issue.

00:46:18.620 --> 00:46:20.820
And he had the encouragement of Robert Kennedy

00:46:20.820 --> 00:46:23.140
to push him in this direction.

00:46:25.440 --> 00:46:27.160
Robert Kennedy was learning.

00:46:27.690 --> 00:46:28.590
He was growing.

00:46:30.030 --> 00:46:37.390
He said to me, John, I now understand the young people have educated me.

00:46:38.480 --> 00:46:40.340
And you can see it, you can feel it.

00:46:41.100 --> 00:46:44.680
And Robert Kennedy, during that period, became so convinced,

00:46:45.460 --> 00:46:48.060
not just as a politician, not just as the Attorney General,

00:46:48.320 --> 00:46:52.960
but as a human being, there was time for that to be some major steps

00:46:52.960 --> 00:46:55.260
to end racial discrimination in America.

00:46:56.890 --> 00:46:59.970
While Bobby increasingly empathized with the civil rights...

00:47:00.000 --> 00:47:07.110
movement. That fall, he authorized a wiretap on Martin Luther King at the request of one of the

00:47:07.110 --> 00:47:15.240
most powerful figures in Washington. FBI Director J. Edgar Hoover hated King, insisted that King

00:47:15.240 --> 00:47:23.220
was a communist threat, and placed him under close surveillance. Bobby acquiesced. If there

00:47:23.220 --> 00:47:28.560
were communists in the civil rights movement, he wanted to know about it. But there was another

00:47:28.560 --> 00:47:28.960
reason.

00:47:30.000 --> 00:47:34.610
Bobby was afraid of Hoover, not for himself, but for his brother.

00:47:35.220 --> 00:47:41.520
Jack was a promiscuous guy who took great pleasure in having a lot of women around him,

00:47:42.050 --> 00:47:48.040
and he was careless about this after he became president, almost to the point of recklessness.

00:47:49.200 --> 00:47:51.300
Bobby knew that Hoover was a blackmail artist.

00:47:52.100 --> 00:47:55.620
He knew that Hoover had files on his brother.

00:47:56.320 --> 00:47:59.940
Hoover had heard that Bobby wanted to fire him, and he wanted to use...

00:48:00.060 --> 00:48:02.680
President Kennedy's sexual habits.

00:48:03.740 --> 00:48:07.300
to essentially blackmail the administration to make sure that he kept his job.

00:48:08.250 --> 00:48:12.410
There's no question Bob regretted that he had authorized the wiretapping of King.

00:48:12.860 --> 00:48:15.160
But Hoover was somebody that everybody...

00:48:15.740 --> 00:48:16.920
was intimidated by.

00:48:17.240 --> 00:48:18.380
If Hoover ever...

00:48:19.740 --> 00:48:23.000
released any of the information he had, allowed it to be known.

00:48:24.340 --> 00:48:26.100
What would happen to the Kennedy presidency?

00:48:33.700 --> 00:48:35.660
On November 20th, 1963,

00:48:36.610 --> 00:48:40.550
Bobby celebrated his 38th birthday at a party at Hickory Hill.

00:48:49.060 --> 00:48:51.140
The president stopped by to wish him well.

00:48:53.770 --> 00:48:56.290
He was about to leave for a political trip to Dallas.

00:48:57.960 --> 00:48:59.780
He said he was looking forward to it.

00:49:02.640 --> 00:49:05.220
Already, the brothers were thinking about the next election.

00:49:05.810 --> 00:49:08.210
and the opportunities a second term would give them.

00:49:10.620 --> 00:49:12.420
Three years of intense collaboration.

00:49:13.270 --> 00:49:14.810
had bound them so closely together.

00:49:15.450 --> 00:49:16.630
Each seemed to know.

00:49:17.110 --> 00:49:18.270
what the other was thinking.

00:49:19.700 --> 00:49:22.460
no sooner that one of them began its sentence than the other.

00:49:22.720 --> 00:49:26.300
knew what he was saying. They understood each other so well.

00:49:27.220 --> 00:49:27.700
that

00:49:28.060 --> 00:49:29.720
They talked in a kind of shorthand.

00:49:33.220 --> 00:49:38.720
Well, everyone was having a good time, it was a birthday party, and the president stopped by, the motorcade came up and...

00:49:39.270 --> 00:49:42.610
He came in and shook hands, and it didn't stay very long as I remember.

00:49:44.100 --> 00:49:46.060
Then he was off to Dallas, I guess, the next day.

00:49:49.740 --> 00:49:51.980
Bobby would never see his brother again.

00:49:55.780 --> 00:49:58.800
on the afternoon of November 22nd, 1963.

00:50:00.220 --> 00:50:03.220
Robert Kennedy received a phone call from J. Edgar Hoover.

00:50:06.360 --> 00:50:08.080
I have news for you, Hoover said.

00:50:08.860 --> 00:50:10.240
The president's been shot.

00:50:13.880 --> 00:50:15.940
30 minutes later, the phone rang again.

00:50:18.080 --> 00:50:18.720
Bobby's brother.

00:50:19.260 --> 00:50:19.760
was dead.

00:50:41.800 --> 00:50:43.960
On November 24th, 1963,

00:50:44.840 --> 00:50:46.640
while the nation mourned a president.

00:50:47.530 --> 00:50:49.390
Robert Kennedy grieved for a brother.

00:50:49.900 --> 00:50:51.320
to whom he had been devoted.

00:50:54.500 --> 00:50:56.660
It was a physical blow to him.

00:50:57.060 --> 00:50:58.080
That loss of his brother.

00:50:59.210 --> 00:50:59.870
an emotional

00:51:00.000 --> 00:51:00.220
blow.

00:51:01.240 --> 00:51:02.040
Lecture will blow.

00:51:03.060 --> 00:51:05.020
But it took a physical toll on him.

00:51:05.180 --> 00:51:06.640
He was physically in pain.

00:51:11.260 --> 00:51:11.800
enormous

00:51:13.020 --> 00:51:13.700
Sadness.

00:51:14.040 --> 00:51:14.540
And...

00:51:14.740 --> 00:51:15.020
Hey!

00:51:15.380 --> 00:51:16.540
It was there all the time.

00:51:18.390 --> 00:51:22.030
I never knew him when he wasn't in a kind of warning for a road.

00:51:22.200 --> 00:51:22.880
President Kennedy.

00:51:23.100 --> 00:51:23.320
You

00:51:28.060 --> 00:51:29.820
with Bobby and I've talked with him for a while.

00:51:30.000 --> 00:51:31.360
and it came time to go to bed.

00:51:32.010 --> 00:51:34.350
So I closed the door and I waited outside and I heard him.

00:51:34.890 --> 00:51:35.890
Just sobbing.

00:51:36.710 --> 00:51:37.530
And he was saying,

00:51:38.120 --> 00:51:38.560
Why?

00:51:39.240 --> 00:51:40.380
Why, God, why?

00:51:53.770 --> 00:51:55.550
I think that Bobby found himself

00:51:56.360 --> 00:51:57.020
be riffed?

00:51:58.790 --> 00:51:59.350
Alone.

00:52:01.020 --> 00:52:09.300
and the inheritor of not only a family ambition, but a national myth.

00:52:19.900 --> 00:52:24.410
When Jack died, Bobby was truly at a loss.

00:52:24.880 --> 00:52:29.970
He was deprived not only of a brother he loved, but in a sense of his own identity.

00:52:30.000 --> 00:52:38.380
Because to be the helper and be the servant, be the henchman, could no longer be his identity.

00:52:38.640 --> 00:52:41.260
He had to forge some kind of identity for himself.

00:52:43.360 --> 00:52:48.220
He didn't know who he was, or what he stood for, or what he was capable of.

00:52:49.400 --> 00:52:51.800
His brother's legacy was his to bear.

00:52:52.980 --> 00:52:57.650
Now, he would have to struggle to create a legacy of his own.

00:53:21.560 --> 00:53:26.730
It would have been difficult for Robert Kennedy to watch any man take his brother's place.

00:53:27.820 --> 00:53:29.970
But watching Lyndon Johnson was next.

00:53:30.000 --> 00:53:42.320
to impossible all i have i would have given gladly not to be standing here today the two men

00:53:42.320 --> 00:53:52.970
now rarely spoke of one another without contempt bobby described lbj as mean bitter vicious lbj

00:53:52.970 --> 00:53:59.780
called bobby a snot-nosed kid and a grandstanding little runt johnson

00:54:00.000 --> 00:54:01.680
had particular contempt for the fact

00:54:01.680 --> 00:54:04.080
that Robert Kennedy had never run for office,

00:54:04.220 --> 00:54:05.620
he had never been elected to anything,

00:54:05.680 --> 00:54:07.780
and he saw him as a child of privilege,

00:54:07.940 --> 00:54:11.520
as someone who had never really earned the offices

00:54:11.520 --> 00:54:13.510
that he held, whereas Lyndon Johnson

00:54:13.510 --> 00:54:15.790
had had to fight for it on his own

00:54:15.790 --> 00:54:17.350
and earned it in his own right.

00:54:18.760 --> 00:54:21.340
LBJ moved quickly to assume the mantle

00:54:21.340 --> 00:54:22.480
of the fallen president.

00:54:22.760 --> 00:54:26.560
He convinced a reluctant Robert Kennedy,

00:54:26.810 --> 00:54:29.090
along with the rest of John Kennedy's cabinet,

00:54:29.330 --> 00:54:29.970
to stay on.

00:54:30.000 --> 00:54:36.000
Then swiftly began to push JFK's sputtering legislative program through Congress.

00:54:37.130 --> 00:54:44.030
My fellow Americans, I'm about to sign into law the Civil Rights Act of 1964.

00:54:44.450 --> 00:54:51.780
The Kennedy legislative program was sitting on the Hill and not doing much of anything by the time of John Kennedy's assassination.

00:54:52.580 --> 00:54:59.980
Lyndon Johnson picks it up, infuses it with energy, gives it new meaning and new strength, and drives it through the Congress like only...

00:55:00.000 --> 00:55:01.020
Lyndon Johnson can.

00:55:01.450 --> 00:55:05.810
This administration today, here, and now,

00:55:07.020 --> 00:55:11.200
declares unconditional war on poverty in America.

00:55:13.690 --> 00:55:16.110
One might expect Robert Kennedy to be pleased

00:55:16.110 --> 00:55:17.910
about the success of the Kennedy agenda.

00:55:18.190 --> 00:55:21.010
But it was hard to be pleased with what LBJ was doing

00:55:21.010 --> 00:55:24.080
when it was LBJ doing it and not his brother.

00:55:25.750 --> 00:55:27.470
He thinks of Johnson as a usurper.

00:55:27.570 --> 00:55:29.670
He thinks of Johnson as illegitimate.

00:55:30.000 --> 00:55:35.820
And it's painful for him to watch Johnson take these pieces of his brother's legacy

00:55:35.820 --> 00:55:38.480
and take ownership of his brother's legacy.

00:55:39.680 --> 00:55:43.440
It no longer belongs to his brother. It no longer belongs to him.

00:55:43.440 --> 00:55:45.660
It belongs to Lyndon Johnson.

00:55:48.760 --> 00:55:54.040
While LBJ dominated the nation's politics, Bobby remained inconsolable.

00:55:55.090 --> 00:55:59.970
He was a haunted man, forever dwelling on his loss, as if cultivating the...

00:56:00.000 --> 00:56:02.670
pain helped keep his brother's memory alive.

00:56:04.440 --> 00:56:06.760
He took to wearing his brother's old jacket,

00:56:07.230 --> 00:56:09.010
although he often forgot it somewhere,

00:56:09.500 --> 00:56:12.740
as if the jacket was at once a connection to his brother

00:56:12.740 --> 00:56:16.860
and a kind of burden he desperately wanted to leave behind.

00:56:17.480 --> 00:56:19.080
Pain was etched on his face.

00:56:19.690 --> 00:56:21.620
He told me he couldn't sleep. He'd lost weight.

00:56:22.220 --> 00:56:24.560
He asked me how he looked. I said, you look like hell.

00:56:24.760 --> 00:56:25.640
And he did.

00:56:26.810 --> 00:56:29.840
He'd become, as in the Robert Frost,

00:56:30.000 --> 00:56:32.520
poem that his brother also often loved to quote,

00:56:32.740 --> 00:56:33.940
Acquainted with the Night.

00:56:38.710 --> 00:56:41.770
He and the president's widow often visited

00:56:41.770 --> 00:56:45.390
John Kennedy's grave, their shared sorrow

00:56:45.390 --> 00:56:46.850
drawing them closer together.

00:56:46.850 --> 00:56:52.360
When his faith in the Catholic Church

00:56:52.360 --> 00:56:56.440
could not alone sustain him, it was Jacqueline Kennedy

00:56:56.440 --> 00:56:59.780
who suggested he read the tragic dramas of the ancient

00:57:00.000 --> 00:57:11.920
Greeks. In agony, learn wisdom, he read in Aeschylus. Injustice is the nature of things.

00:57:14.780 --> 00:57:22.240
Grief humanized him. I think it took him away from a life dedicated to power and will

00:57:22.240 --> 00:57:29.050
and ambition toward a deeper identification.

00:57:30.190 --> 00:57:32.410
with people who suffered.

00:57:34.150 --> 00:57:36.750
Slowly, Bobby emerged from his despair,

00:57:37.540 --> 00:57:40.300
reconnecting to the everyday world through politics

00:57:40.300 --> 00:57:43.980
and his need to carry forth the legacy of his brother.

00:57:47.900 --> 00:57:51.180
Over the past few weeks, many leading members

00:57:51.180 --> 00:57:53.000
of the Democratic and the Liberal parties

00:57:53.000 --> 00:57:56.390
here in the state of New York have talked to me

00:57:56.390 --> 00:57:59.040
about being a candidate for the United States Senate.

00:58:00.000 --> 00:58:06.260
On August 22nd, 1964, still wearing the black tie he had worn since his brother's death,

00:58:07.030 --> 00:58:11.370
Robert Kennedy announced that for the first time, he would run for political office.

00:58:12.170 --> 00:58:15.590
I shall resign from the cabinet to campaign for election.

00:58:16.470 --> 00:58:22.880
I shall devote all of my effort and whatever talents that I possess to the state of New York.

00:58:23.550 --> 00:58:24.170
This I pledge.

00:58:27.440 --> 00:58:29.600
It is the biggest transition of his life.

00:58:30.000 --> 00:58:32.340
to go from the shadows to the stage.

00:58:32.860 --> 00:58:35.620
And he had a fear, what if I lose?

00:58:36.110 --> 00:58:37.790
What if I let my brother down?

00:58:38.260 --> 00:58:39.840
What if I let my family down?

00:58:39.940 --> 00:58:41.620
What if I've made the wrong decision?

00:58:41.900 --> 00:58:43.880
What if I'm not ready to run for the Senate?

00:58:45.400 --> 00:58:49.170
He was still a wounded animal, half a zombie.

00:58:50.030 --> 00:58:53.500
He wasn't ready to face the voters.

00:58:53.890 --> 00:58:55.570
Former Attorney General of the United States

00:58:55.570 --> 00:58:58.090
and candidate for the United States Senate from New York,

00:58:58.240 --> 00:58:59.060
Robert F. Kennedy.

00:59:01.400 --> 00:59:03.320
He was still hurting and suffering.

00:59:06.870 --> 00:59:09.030
Although voters turned out in large numbers,

00:59:09.710 --> 00:59:12.090
Bobby found it hard to accept their enthusiasm.

00:59:14.260 --> 00:59:16.040
They're cheering him, he said.

00:59:16.980 --> 00:59:17.800
They're for him.

00:59:18.770 --> 00:59:22.650
He didn't want to trade on being his brother's brother.

00:59:22.730 --> 00:59:23.990
He didn't think that was right.

00:59:24.090 --> 00:59:29.970
At the same time, he didn't really have a series of things that I...

00:59:30.090 --> 00:59:32.430
Kennedy will do as the senator from the state of New York.

00:59:32.590 --> 00:59:34.950
What about this tour of the Fulton Fish Market?

00:59:35.170 --> 00:59:36.050
What are your impressions?

00:59:36.130 --> 00:59:38.530
They have a lot of fish.

00:59:39.110 --> 00:59:41.340
And it just didn't work very well.

00:59:41.560 --> 00:59:47.550
I am very pleased and happy to have the opportunity

00:59:47.550 --> 00:59:50.870
to speak with all of you this morning.

00:59:51.490 --> 00:59:53.570
He quickly fell behind in the polls.

00:59:54.530 --> 00:59:57.670
He was struggling to define himself and what he stood for.

00:59:58.140 --> 00:59:59.560
I join...

00:59:59.560 --> 00:59:59.820
Thank you very much.

01:00:00.000 --> 01:00:05.000
Senator Bob Brownstein, Congressman Multa.

01:00:05.720 --> 01:00:08.580
He has spoken for his brother, on behalf of his brother,

01:00:08.700 --> 01:00:10.180
for most of his political life.

01:00:10.670 --> 01:00:12.410
Suddenly he's a figure in his own right,

01:00:12.410 --> 01:00:14.690
but he can really only see himself

01:00:14.690 --> 01:00:16.430
as fulfilling his brother's legacy,

01:00:16.930 --> 01:00:18.470
doing what his brother would do

01:00:18.470 --> 01:00:20.150
if only his brother were still with us.

01:00:23.940 --> 01:00:27.320
It was started January 1961, and I took this election.

01:00:27.660 --> 01:00:28.780
The letter was going to keep it going.

01:00:30.540 --> 01:00:33.380
He quoted his brother almost obsessively.

01:00:33.560 --> 01:00:36.140
He had a lot of the hand gestures of his brother.

01:00:36.280 --> 01:00:38.120
And it was easy to believe for a minute,

01:00:38.180 --> 01:00:40.200
and you didn't have to close your eyes to do it,

01:00:40.500 --> 01:00:42.260
that John Kennedy was still with us.

01:00:42.850 --> 01:00:44.370
We want to continue that effort,

01:00:44.490 --> 01:00:46.410
then we have to elect the Democratic administration.

01:00:48.410 --> 01:00:50.190
Bobby was at war with himself,

01:00:50.640 --> 01:00:52.400
his need to emulate his brother,

01:00:52.840 --> 01:00:55.820
battling with his desire to find his own voice.

01:00:56.600 --> 01:00:59.410
I think that this election's important.

01:01:00.000 --> 01:01:04.810
I think that there's a lot that needs to be done in a horrible situation, and it has to be changed.

01:01:04.990 --> 01:01:08.110
If I'm elected to the United States Senate, I intend to fight for that.

01:01:08.390 --> 01:01:14.430
Despite the cheering crowds, as the campaign wound down, the election remained too close to Cole.

01:01:18.730 --> 01:01:23.180
Much to Kennedy's chagrin, there was only one man who could help him.

01:01:23.620 --> 01:01:28.720
The United States needs a young dynamic.

01:01:30.480 --> 01:01:36.750
compassionate, fighting liberal, representing New York in the United States Senate, Bob Kennedy.

01:01:37.320 --> 01:01:44.280
I had to stick in RFK's craw to have to accept anything from Lyndon Johnson.

01:01:46.420 --> 01:01:50.460
Bobby had trouble hiding his distaste, but he was a pragmatist.

01:01:51.920 --> 01:01:56.310
LBJ may have hated Bobby, but he wanted a Democrat in the Senate.

01:02:00.730 --> 01:02:05.740
When all the votes were counted, LBJ had helped put Bobby over the top.

01:02:06.280 --> 01:02:10.900
For all of us who are elected on this day, all of us now have a responsibility.

01:02:11.240 --> 01:02:12.460
Our job has just begun.

01:02:12.780 --> 01:02:19.720
But even victory could not relieve his melancholy.

01:02:22.320 --> 01:02:26.560
If my brother was alive, Bobby told a friend, I wouldn't be here.

01:02:27.920 --> 01:02:29.240
I'd rather have it that way.

01:02:32.240 --> 01:02:36.070
That night, Bobby called LBJ to thank him for his help.

01:02:36.590 --> 01:02:40.050
We've got a lot to be thankful for, Bobby, and give our love to Ethel,

01:02:40.150 --> 01:02:44.150
and let's stay as close together as he'd want us to.

01:02:44.390 --> 01:02:45.530
That'd be fine. That'd be fine.

01:02:45.630 --> 01:02:46.610
President, congratulations.

01:02:46.890 --> 01:02:50.290
Tell all that staff of yours that ain't nobody going to divide us,

01:02:50.330 --> 01:02:55.750
and I'll tell mine the same way, and we'll move ahead, and I'm proud of you.

01:02:55.890 --> 01:02:57.410
Thanks very much, and thanks for your help.

01:02:57.570 --> 01:02:58.390
Thank you for calling.

01:02:58.530 --> 01:02:59.230
Made a hell of a difference.

01:03:08.060 --> 01:03:10.660
Bobby Kennedy was never cut out to be a senator.

01:03:11.400 --> 01:03:13.160
He was used to making things happen,

01:03:13.160 --> 01:03:16.620
and the Senate's glacial pace irritated him.

01:03:16.760 --> 01:03:18.540
He was the second most powerful man

01:03:18.540 --> 01:03:20.200
in the most powerful country in the world,

01:03:20.220 --> 01:03:21.520
and now he's a junior senator.

01:03:21.760 --> 01:03:23.880
He can't even get a seat on the Foreign Relations Committee.

01:03:24.080 --> 01:03:25.500
He used to run covert actions,

01:03:25.540 --> 01:03:27.140
and now he can't even go to the hearings.

01:03:27.640 --> 01:03:29.960
So he is a grumpy, cranky...

01:03:30.000 --> 01:03:34.160
senator who won't sit through long-winded speeches and is disrespectful of other senators.

01:03:34.300 --> 01:03:41.780
He'll just get up and walk out. Bobby, a friend said, felt impotent, frustrated, floundering.

01:03:42.840 --> 01:03:48.360
He escaped the Senate chambers as often as he could, channeling his restless energies elsewhere.

01:03:51.510 --> 01:03:57.420
Two months after taking his seat in the Senate, he set out to scale a 14,000-foot mountain in

01:03:57.420 --> 01:03:59.960
Canada, recently named Mount Kennedy.

01:04:00.000 --> 01:04:08.480
after his brother. No one had ever made it to the top before, and Robert Kennedy had never

01:04:08.480 --> 01:04:15.150
climbed a mountain before. Bobby hated heights. He was afraid of heights. He didn't train for it,

01:04:15.210 --> 01:04:18.750
really. I mean, he joked that, as Ethel said, that he trained for it by running up and down

01:04:18.750 --> 01:04:28.230
the stairs yelling, help. He was nearly 40 years old. Now, roped between two veteran climbers,

01:04:28.290 --> 01:04:29.980
Bobby forced his way

01:04:30.000 --> 01:04:33.430
forward, urging the others to increase the pace.

01:04:36.780 --> 01:04:39.540
I think Robert Kennedy had a reckless streak in him.

01:04:40.360 --> 01:04:46.810
He was a ferocious competitor and with tremendous amount of aggression and will.

01:05:01.400 --> 01:05:08.120
After two days of climbing, nearing the top, Bobby moved ahead and reached the summit alone.

01:05:14.440 --> 01:05:18.780
Kneeling down, he wedged his brother's inaugural address into the snow.

01:05:25.410 --> 01:05:27.960
For a moment, he stared off into the distance.

01:05:30.120 --> 01:05:33.150
I didn't really enjoy any part of it, he said later.

01:05:36.910 --> 01:05:42.220
It was a daring exploit and a media sensation that would have delighted any politician.

01:05:42.480 --> 01:05:47.380
But American politics were about to take a darker turn.

01:05:48.540 --> 01:05:53.350
Bombs had begun to fall on a small, impoverished country in Southeast Asia.

01:06:01.820 --> 01:06:06.380
Lyndon Johnson began bombing North Vietnam in February 1965.

01:06:11.070 --> 01:06:17.010
He had inherited the war from Bobby's brother. Now he was escalating it.

01:06:19.700 --> 01:06:24.300
With Vietnam divided into a communist North and an American-supported South,

01:06:25.070 --> 01:06:29.970
LBJ hoped that massive air assaults would force the North Vietnamese to abandon

01:06:30.000 --> 01:06:37.700
to reunite the country, but the North Vietnamese resisted.

01:06:41.890 --> 01:06:45.160
For the next three years, Robert Kennedy's fate

01:06:45.160 --> 01:06:48.360
would be increasingly bound to the outcome of a war

01:06:48.360 --> 01:06:50.370
on the other side of the world.

01:06:54.480 --> 01:06:56.140
Bobby had been committed to the war

01:06:56.140 --> 01:06:57.560
when his brother was president.

01:06:58.380 --> 01:06:59.980
Now that LBJ was in the White House,

01:07:00.000 --> 01:07:07.580
House, he continued to support its objectives. The loss of Vietnam, he told an aide, would mean the

01:07:07.580 --> 01:07:13.270
loss of all of Southeast Asia and have profound effects on our position throughout the world.

01:07:14.120 --> 01:07:19.380
Bobby is a cold warrior. He shares the worldview that communism is a threat and it's advancing and

01:07:19.380 --> 01:07:23.460
that you have to contain it. So he doesn't question the underlying assumptions of the

01:07:23.460 --> 01:07:26.680
Vietnam War. He embraces them and endorses them.

01:07:37.550 --> 01:07:41.450
But Robert Kennedy is deeply uneasy about Johnson's escalation of the war,

01:07:41.570 --> 01:07:42.810
really from the very beginning.

01:07:43.110 --> 01:07:49.310
He speaks to Johnson privately on the telephone,

01:07:49.540 --> 01:07:51.040
conversations that were recorded.

01:07:51.950 --> 01:07:56.200
I have not been involved intimately with Southeast Asia or Vietnam,

01:07:56.520 --> 01:07:59.700
but I would think that that war will never be won militarily.

01:08:00.030 --> 01:08:06.160
But where it's going to be won, really, is that military action obviously will have to be taken,

01:08:06.510 --> 01:08:08.970
unless the political action is taken place.

01:08:10.400 --> 01:08:12.830
In my judgment, I just don't think it can be successful.

01:08:14.210 --> 01:08:22.270
I think that that's not any different from the way I have about it.

01:08:22.930 --> 01:08:26.070
Johnson says that he agrees with Bobby Kennedy completely,

01:08:26.070 --> 01:08:29.970
and this is the sort of thing that Johnson probably believes when he's saying it.

01:08:30.280 --> 01:08:32.700
but it is not the course of U.S. policy.

01:08:35.210 --> 01:08:40.770
All through the spring of 1965, Johnson continued to up the ante.

01:08:42.360 --> 01:08:46.140
He had already sent 75,000 American soldiers to Vietnam.

01:08:48.010 --> 01:08:50.760
That July, he sent 50,000 more.

01:08:51.000 --> 01:08:54.890
As the war drifted out of control,

01:08:55.690 --> 01:08:58.850
Bobby began to tentatively air his doubts in public.

01:09:00.190 --> 01:09:04.370
you the direct question, do you support fully his present policy on Vietnam?

01:09:07.620 --> 01:09:14.460
Well, I support, I basically support the policy, uh, Spivak. I have some reservations about whether

01:09:14.460 --> 01:09:20.240
we're doing enough in the economic and the political field, and I also have felt for some

01:09:20.240 --> 01:09:25.800
period of time that, uh, a major effort had to be undertaken in the diplomatic field.

01:09:25.990 --> 01:09:29.690
Robert Kennedy believes that America needs to bring Ho Chi Minh.

01:09:30.060 --> 01:09:31.480
to the table to negotiate.

01:09:31.740 --> 01:09:34.020
Johnson, meanwhile, has taken the opposite course,

01:09:34.220 --> 01:09:37.260
which is to escalate, to try to bomb Ho into submission.

01:09:38.520 --> 01:09:42.760
For the rest of 1966, Bobby said little about the war.

01:09:43.580 --> 01:09:46.740
Instead, he turned his attention to problems at home.

01:09:47.590 --> 01:09:50.970
He would begin to speak out on behalf of the dispossessed,

01:09:51.520 --> 01:09:53.160
and as he reached out to them,

01:09:53.740 --> 01:09:56.240
he would transform his sense of himself.

01:10:06.040 --> 01:10:12.990
In the summer of 1965, riots in Watts, a poor African-American section of Los Angeles,

01:10:13.700 --> 01:10:17.540
left 34 people dead and more than 1,000 injured.

01:10:19.630 --> 01:10:20.910
Bobby was shaken.

01:10:23.200 --> 01:10:27.420
He bumped into some reporter who asked him what he thought about, you know,

01:10:27.540 --> 01:10:29.960
should these Negroes, as we called them then, be...

01:10:30.000 --> 01:10:35.140
obeying the law, and he said, well, he said, I don't know, he said, what did the law ever do for

01:10:35.140 --> 01:10:41.160
the Negro? I don't think that it's possible in our society and with our government to tolerate

01:10:41.580 --> 01:10:46.880
lawlessness and disorder and violence, but at the same time, I think that we've got to

01:10:46.880 --> 01:10:52.140
make more progress than we have in the past, be more effective with the programs that we've

01:10:52.140 --> 01:10:58.770
instituted, have some imagination to try to deal with the lack of hope.

01:11:00.000 --> 01:11:10.850
that exists in many of these communities in a neighborhood in brooklyn called bedford stuyvesant

01:11:11.320 --> 01:11:20.580
bobby kennedy meant to show the nation what could be done about poverty bedford stuyvesant has got

01:11:20.580 --> 01:11:29.520
tremendous problems of unemployment drugs slum housing robert kennedy was an activist he wanted to

01:11:30.000 --> 01:11:35.620
something about it. He felt it was cheap grace to just make a speech and deplore

01:11:35.620 --> 01:11:41.600
poverty or racism and not do something about it. And so it gets involved with

01:11:41.600 --> 01:11:46.760
the community leadership and they plan together an initiative that will be not

01:11:46.840 --> 01:11:52.370
only about housing in the neighborhood but about jobs and about public safety

01:11:52.370 --> 01:11:56.550
and about really trying to build a community there. What we brought together

01:11:56.550 --> 01:11:58.940
for the first time, really, is the city.

01:12:00.000 --> 01:12:06.150
foundations the federal government and I think most significant the private

01:12:06.150 --> 01:12:10.720
sector when he went to the business people he didn't say I want you to give

01:12:10.720 --> 01:12:17.680
money I want charity because that's too easy that just buys off what he did was

01:12:17.680 --> 01:12:22.820
he said I want investment here I want you to run business here I want you to

01:12:22.820 --> 01:12:27.440
hire people I want you to make things here to have any success in Bedford

01:12:27.440 --> 01:12:29.900
Stuyvesant is going to require the initiative and the imagination.

01:12:30.000 --> 01:12:32.910
and the courage of the people who live in the community.

01:12:33.750 --> 01:12:35.610
The programs are going to have to be developed

01:12:35.610 --> 01:12:37.090
by the people who live in the community.

01:12:37.530 --> 01:12:40.410
He believed the core of any program

01:12:40.410 --> 01:12:44.750
had to be work and self-help and self-mastery.

01:12:45.780 --> 01:12:48.280
People had to have the wherewithal

01:12:48.280 --> 01:12:50.100
not to be defined by others,

01:12:50.280 --> 01:12:52.280
but to be able to define themselves.

01:12:53.190 --> 01:12:54.590
America's a very rich country.

01:12:54.690 --> 01:12:56.650
We could just hand out incomes.

01:12:57.090 --> 01:12:59.710
What he said was that that would be profoundly...

01:13:00.000 --> 01:13:06.470
destructive. He hated welfare, but thought jobs were the solution, not just throwing people off

01:13:06.470 --> 01:13:14.640
the rolls. He had this empathy, seeing it from the point of view of what it would like to be,

01:13:14.820 --> 01:13:21.380
a black child in a desperate situation with no jobs and no chance of college and broken family

01:13:21.380 --> 01:13:29.820
and drugs and gangs all around. I grew up in Bedford-Stuyvesant, and I'll never forget one of the

01:13:29.820 --> 01:13:29.960
day.

01:13:30.000 --> 01:13:35.780
were going around Bed-Stuy, he said to me, I envy the fact you grew up in poverty and

01:13:35.960 --> 01:13:41.250
you grew up with black friends. And I said, well, what would have happened to you if you

01:13:41.250 --> 01:13:46.610
had grown up on this block instead of me? And he looks up and down the block at all of this

01:13:46.610 --> 01:13:53.270
poverty and drug addiction and alcoholism. And he says, if I grew up here, I would have

01:13:53.270 --> 01:13:56.130
either become a juvenile delinquent or a revolutionary.

01:13:56.130 --> 01:13:59.980
Robert Kennedy had begun to

01:14:00.120 --> 01:14:02.600
beyond the guarded politics of his brother,

01:14:04.160 --> 01:14:07.230
John Kennedy had always moved slowly, with caution.

01:14:08.420 --> 01:14:11.960
Now, Bobby was throwing caution to the wind.

01:14:17.760 --> 01:14:21.630
In March 1966, Kennedy flew to California,

01:14:21.850 --> 01:14:24.090
where his Senate subcommittee was investigating

01:14:24.090 --> 01:14:27.300
the causes of a strike by migrant grape pickers.

01:14:29.480 --> 01:14:29.980
Robert...

01:14:30.000 --> 01:14:36.810
Kennedy walks into the hearings and the witness is this sheriff who is explaining to the committee

01:14:36.810 --> 01:14:43.360
how it is that he's arresting these demonstrators who are legally picketing.

01:14:43.760 --> 01:14:48.100
If I have reason to believe that there's going to be a riot started and somebody tells me

01:14:48.100 --> 01:14:52.560
that there's going to be trouble if you don't stop them, then it's my duty to stop them.

01:14:52.700 --> 01:14:53.860
Then you go out and arrest them?

01:14:54.270 --> 01:14:54.750
Absolutely.

01:14:55.070 --> 01:14:57.190
How can you go arrest somebody if they haven't violated the law?

01:14:57.690 --> 01:14:59.310
They're ready to violate the law.

01:14:59.450 --> 01:14:59.970
In other words...

01:15:02.650 --> 01:15:06.450
Just like these labor people out here, they ask their attorney, what shall we do?

01:15:06.570 --> 01:15:12.800
Could I suggest in the luncheon period of time that the sheriff and the district attorney read the Constitution of the United States?

01:15:18.620 --> 01:15:23.070
Bobby declared his support for the grape strike, even joined a picket line.

01:15:24.160 --> 01:15:29.710
He was discovering there were causes he believed in, people he could fight for.

01:15:30.440 --> 01:15:32.400
Kennedy took things personally.

01:15:32.660 --> 01:15:34.960
He saw somebody hurting and he hurt.

01:15:35.190 --> 01:15:38.830
He was so intense, so personal

01:15:38.830 --> 01:15:42.190
about somebody else's pain or injustice.

01:15:42.940 --> 01:15:45.400
And that's what made him a totally different

01:15:45.400 --> 01:15:46.280
kind of senator.

01:15:46.480 --> 01:15:59.980
By the beginning of 1967, almost 400...

01:16:00.000 --> 01:16:02.400
100,000 Americans were fighting in Vietnam.

01:16:07.760 --> 01:16:09.780
More than 9,000 had been killed.

01:16:11.580 --> 01:16:13.430
More than 60,000 wounded.

01:16:16.980 --> 01:16:20.200
As the carnage continued, senators and congressmen

01:16:20.200 --> 01:16:22.140
from Lyndon Johnson's own party

01:16:22.140 --> 01:16:24.320
began to speak out against the war.

01:16:27.330 --> 01:16:29.360
Bobby Kennedy still hesitated.

01:16:30.000 --> 01:16:34.920
It took Kennedy a long time to decide he was going to oppose the Vietnam War.

01:16:35.670 --> 01:16:38.570
It was an agonizing process of indecision.

01:16:40.460 --> 01:16:44.580
He values courage above all other human qualities,

01:16:44.600 --> 01:16:47.320
and he realizes he's not displaying courage.

01:16:47.320 --> 01:16:50.180
He's not displaying leadership.

01:16:51.400 --> 01:16:53.360
But he was conflicted.

01:16:58.400 --> 01:16:59.980
Not until February 19...

01:17:00.000 --> 01:17:06.700
1967 would Kennedy break decisively with the president after a bitter confrontation in the

01:17:06.700 --> 01:17:13.210
White House? Robert Kennedy takes a trip to Europe in January of 1967, and he comes back to find that

01:17:13.210 --> 01:17:18.690
there's a report in Newsweek that he has received an important peace feeler, as it was called, from

01:17:18.690 --> 01:17:23.790
the Vietnamese, that they are trying to send a plan for peace through him. Now, there's no truth

01:17:23.790 --> 01:17:29.970
to this whatsoever, but there it is in Newsweek. And LBJ called and asked if we both could go.

01:17:30.000 --> 01:17:36.140
to the white house which we did johnson was in a foul mood and he was embarrassed by it and he was

01:17:36.640 --> 01:17:41.220
he was always prepared to think that anything that he was embarrassed by was something that bobby

01:17:41.220 --> 01:17:51.670
had done lbj was just terrible he was mean and nasty to bobby and bobby was saying i had nothing

01:17:51.670 --> 01:17:56.930
to do with this i don't know of any peace fielders that were made and johnson believed none of it

01:17:57.880 --> 01:17:59.980
And Bobby was just li-

01:18:00.060 --> 01:18:01.160
but he was so mad.

01:18:01.820 --> 01:18:04.880
And Bobby comes back to his office and says,

01:18:05.410 --> 01:18:06.570
the president's unhinged.

01:18:06.670 --> 01:18:10.430
I mean, he was abusive and maybe just not mentally stable.

01:18:10.610 --> 01:18:12.410
He told his aides that he would never again

01:18:12.410 --> 01:18:14.090
have anything to do with Lyndon Johnson,

01:18:14.290 --> 01:18:16.850
that the way he had been treated was so inexcusable

01:18:16.850 --> 01:18:20.230
that he could simply never have any real dealings

01:18:20.230 --> 01:18:21.430
with the man past that point.

01:18:22.860 --> 01:18:25.260
A month later, Bobby rose in the Senate,

01:18:25.870 --> 01:18:27.670
condemned the morality of the war,

01:18:28.170 --> 01:18:29.850
and then admitted his own share.

01:18:30.000 --> 01:18:36.780
of responsibility. I can testify, he said, that if fault is to be found, there is enough to go

01:18:36.780 --> 01:18:44.650
around for all, including myself. He is the first politician of either party to take responsibility

01:18:44.650 --> 01:18:51.270
for what's happening in Vietnam. The first politician to accept blame, which gives a moral strength to

01:18:51.270 --> 01:18:55.920
the argument that he's making. Do we have the right here in the United States to say that we're

01:18:55.920 --> 01:18:59.960
going to kill tens of thousands, make millions of people as we have?

01:19:00.030 --> 01:19:04.020
Millions of people, refugees, kill women and children as we have.

01:19:04.440 --> 01:19:07.230
I very seriously question whether we have that right.

01:19:07.660 --> 01:19:10.780
Now we're saying we're going to fight there so that we don't have to fight in Thailand,

01:19:11.520 --> 01:19:14.000
so that we don't have to fight on the west coast of the United States

01:19:14.000 --> 01:19:15.660
so that they won't move across the Rockies.

01:19:15.840 --> 01:19:20.680
But do we, our whole moral position, it seems to me, changes tremendously.

01:19:21.040 --> 01:19:26.160
Robert Kennedy in 1967 has come to question the basic assumptions of the war.

01:19:26.440 --> 01:19:29.900
He has begun to question whether we really do need to make a stand in Vietnam.

01:19:30.000 --> 01:19:31.880
to protect that region from communism.

01:19:32.220 --> 01:19:35.840
He's come to question whether our national security interest in Vietnam

01:19:35.840 --> 01:19:39.140
is outweighed by the incredible human suffering

01:19:39.140 --> 01:19:41.540
that we're inflicting by waging war in this country.

01:19:41.860 --> 01:19:44.840
He's questioning the moral legitimacy of this war,

01:19:44.960 --> 01:19:47.180
which is something that he hasn't done to this point.

01:19:49.710 --> 01:19:51.290
He's confronted by new issues,

01:19:51.310 --> 01:19:54.290
and he grows in order to be able to face these challenges.

01:19:55.330 --> 01:19:56.990
He really learns from experience,

01:19:57.170 --> 01:19:59.910
and he really becomes something much larger than what he was.

01:20:00.000 --> 01:20:07.500
when he began. He becomes more and more thoughtful, more and more philosophical, more and more ready

01:20:07.500 --> 01:20:15.240
to accept that he does not have a monopoly on wisdom, that maybe this war in Vietnam was a

01:20:15.240 --> 01:20:22.960
fundamental mistake, and that maybe his brother would have gotten out. Most people acquire

01:20:22.960 --> 01:20:29.980
certainties as they grow older. Bobby Kennedy discarded certainties. He grew. He started his

01:20:30.000 --> 01:20:36.960
this zealot and he ended up as this man very sympathetic to those who were the despised and

01:20:36.960 --> 01:20:44.880
rejected of life robert kennedy was now reaching out to americans everywhere who had been left

01:20:44.880 --> 01:20:57.830
behind he had fully awakened from his dark night of mourning the moral impulse to fight evil and do

01:20:57.830 --> 01:20:59.970
good that had always been a part of

01:21:00.000 --> 01:21:02.610
was taking a new direction.

01:21:02.930 --> 01:21:07.580
He wanted to know what life was like for someone else.

01:21:08.380 --> 01:21:11.180
He would ask, what do you feel? What do you think?

01:21:12.480 --> 01:21:17.890
He wanted to be inside the eyes of America's casualties.

01:21:18.330 --> 01:21:22.030
He wanted to see the world the way they saw it.

01:21:23.870 --> 01:21:27.490
People believed in his understanding of their situation

01:21:27.830 --> 01:21:29.840
because he visibly was moved.

01:21:30.000 --> 01:21:34.980
He was someone who responded in the most graphic, human, emotional terms.

01:21:35.280 --> 01:21:41.710
He was somebody who bled, who was raw from the hurt done to others.

01:21:42.100 --> 01:21:51.050
The hurt he felt from the assassination of his brother gave him that empathy for everyone else who hurt after that.

01:21:51.540 --> 01:21:58.810
I think he did begin to change incrementally while he was attorney general, before his brother was killed.

01:21:58.830 --> 01:21:59.890
But I think that all...

01:22:02.240 --> 01:22:05.540
grew dramatically after his brother's murder

01:22:05.540 --> 01:22:10.010
because then he identified with every other victim.

01:22:11.480 --> 01:22:14.970
Anyone who was a casualty in life,

01:22:15.150 --> 01:22:17.710
he began to feel was his brother.

01:22:31.690 --> 01:22:34.980
By 1967, America was in turmoil.

01:22:38.470 --> 01:22:41.850
Protesters against the war in Vietnam marched on Washington

01:22:41.850 --> 01:22:44.180
and bitterly attacked the president.

01:22:45.900 --> 01:22:47.880
With the election just one year away,

01:22:48.120 --> 01:22:50.580
they were desperate for someone to challenge him.

01:22:52.490 --> 01:22:55.070
Bobby Kennedy was besieged from all sides

01:22:55.070 --> 01:22:57.430
by people urging him to take on Johnson.

01:22:58.330 --> 01:22:59.290
But he hesitated.

01:23:00.000 --> 01:23:07.720
profoundly conflicted. If I run, Bobby said, I will go a long way toward proving everything that

01:23:07.720 --> 01:23:12.920
everybody who doesn't like me has said about me, that I've never accepted Lyndon Johnson as

01:23:12.920 --> 01:23:18.960
president, that I'm just a selfish, ambitious little SOB. I can't wait to get his hands on

01:23:18.960 --> 01:23:24.760
the White House. He is absolutely tormented by this decision he has to make, either to support

01:23:24.760 --> 01:23:29.980
johnson were to run for president i said to him don't run i said to

01:23:30.000 --> 01:23:35.600
him, you're not going to unseat Lyndon Johnson. He despises you. There's no way he's going to

01:23:35.600 --> 01:23:41.310
step aside if you get in. He'd love to take you to the convention and beat you. But he was caught

01:23:41.310 --> 01:23:47.370
in an emotional trap. It was as if inexorable forces were pushing him in the direction of

01:23:47.370 --> 01:23:57.540
running. And everything he was about in life was suddenly on the line. He was tortured about it.

01:24:00.000 --> 01:24:28.560
I remember talking to him and he suddenly, this gusher came out about his feelings about Johnson and he said I know he's president today because my brother appointed him, nominated him for vice president. I know my brother is implicated in the beginning of the Vietnam War. For me to run against Johnson, I have to run against President Kennedy's judgment. And that was agonizing, agonizing for Robert Kennedy.

01:24:30.000 --> 01:24:32.160
in a way it would have meant running against.

01:24:32.680 --> 01:24:35.420
the person he loved most in the world, his brother.

01:24:37.770 --> 01:24:41.450
Isn't it true, people who believe that you are the real alternative to President Johnson

01:24:41.450 --> 01:24:42.730
are going to bring pressure upon you?

01:24:42.980 --> 01:24:44.620
and increase air activities on your behalf.

01:24:44.900 --> 01:24:45.300
Bye.

01:24:45.670 --> 01:24:47.290
Don't anticipate that that will occur.

01:24:48.260 --> 01:24:48.880
but

01:24:51.180 --> 01:24:52.460
I'm going to continue as I am.

01:24:53.100 --> 01:24:53.720
said and I'm

01:24:53.960 --> 01:24:54.960
not a candidate for this.

01:24:55.270 --> 01:24:56.170
Democratic nomination.

01:24:56.530 --> 01:24:56.610
Senator.

01:24:57.470 --> 01:24:59.770
You know, no matter what I do, I'm difficult.

01:25:00.180 --> 01:25:02.180
Well, I wasn't trying to get you to say that you were a candidate.

01:25:02.360 --> 01:25:06.180
I know you weren't, but you say, isn't that what is going to happen?

01:25:06.870 --> 01:25:09.030
I suppose all kinds of things can happen.

01:25:09.210 --> 01:25:10.330
I don't know what I can do to...

01:25:11.280 --> 01:25:15.420
Prevent that of what I should do that is any different other than to try to get off the earth in some way

01:25:16.440 --> 01:25:18.700
Senator, nobody wants you to get off the earth, obviously.

01:25:18.880 --> 01:25:19.380
Oh, don't do that.

01:25:19.820 --> 01:25:21.720
Nobody's trying to put you on the spot, really.

01:25:21.940 --> 01:25:21.980
No.

01:25:22.140 --> 01:25:24.640
Everyone appreciates the difficulty of your position.

01:25:27.290 --> 01:25:28.810
the hottest places in hell.

01:25:29.080 --> 01:25:29.940
Bobby liked to say.

01:25:30.000 --> 01:25:30.780
quoting Dante.

01:25:31.560 --> 01:25:33.080
are reserved for those who

01:25:33.290 --> 01:25:35.170
in a time of great moral crisis.

01:25:36.040 --> 01:25:37.620
maintain their neutrality.

01:25:41.290 --> 01:25:42.610
While Bobby hesitated,

01:25:43.140 --> 01:25:44.320
Another liberal Democrat

01:25:44.650 --> 01:25:46.770
Minnesota Senator Eugene McCarthy.

01:25:47.370 --> 01:25:50.490
picked up the sword that Kennedy was still refusing to wield.

01:25:51.340 --> 01:25:54.560
I intend to enter the Democratic primaries in...

01:25:55.270 --> 01:25:55.810
four states.

01:25:57.000 --> 01:25:59.540
As McCarthy campaigned through New Hampshire that winter,

01:26:00.310 --> 01:26:02.730
Bobby watched with dismay and envy.

01:26:03.380 --> 01:26:05.800
It hurt Kennedy that he was on the sidelines.

01:26:06.390 --> 01:26:09.930
More and more college students were not just going to McCarthy's campaign,

01:26:10.090 --> 01:26:11.270
but were starting to heckle him.

01:26:11.900 --> 01:26:16.720
I mean, he spoke at Brooklyn college one day and it was a big sign RFK.

01:26:17.440 --> 01:26:18.520
Hawk, dove, or chicken?

01:26:19.540 --> 01:26:20.020
and that

01:26:20.470 --> 01:26:21.710
with a razor in his heart.

01:26:22.580 --> 01:26:25.940
He was really reeling around about what's the right thing to do.

01:26:26.680 --> 01:26:29.460
but he did not see how it's practical to run.

01:26:30.220 --> 01:26:31.120
He took a poll.

01:26:32.070 --> 01:26:32.970
In New Hampshire.

01:26:33.580 --> 01:26:35.320
in January of

01:26:35.970 --> 01:26:36.870
of that year.

01:26:37.960 --> 01:26:43.160
that showed Johnson beating him there 67 to 9 or something like that.

01:26:43.820 --> 01:26:45.680
That was a pretty convincing argument.

01:26:48.140 --> 01:26:49.920
at the end of January 1968.

01:26:50.950 --> 01:26:54.050
a miserable Robert Kennedy finally reached a decision.

01:26:55.050 --> 01:26:57.450
He would not, he told the National Press Club.

01:26:57.830 --> 01:26:59.990
Oppose the president for the Democratic...

01:27:00.000 --> 01:27:03.180
under any foreseeable circumstances.

01:27:13.050 --> 01:27:14.310
that very same day.

01:27:14.870 --> 01:27:17.850
Vietnamese communists launched a series of attacks

01:27:17.850 --> 01:27:20.450
that made him bitterly regret his decision.

01:27:22.080 --> 01:27:25.180
232 GIs killed and 900 wounded.

01:27:25.750 --> 01:27:27.030
is just over two days.

01:27:27.350 --> 01:27:28.350
The past two days.

01:27:28.820 --> 01:27:29.980
two of the worst we have known.

01:27:30.000 --> 01:27:30.640
alone in Vietnam.

01:27:37.380 --> 01:27:42.300
The widespread fighting convinced many Americans that the war was far from over.

01:27:47.590 --> 01:27:49.850
frustrated with the seemingly endless fighting.

01:27:50.730 --> 01:27:52.950
more and more turned away from Johnson.

01:27:53.740 --> 01:27:54.880
to Eugene McCarthy.

01:27:58.470 --> 01:27:59.070
A big surprise.

01:27:59.500 --> 01:28:00.000
The first...

01:28:00.000 --> 01:28:05.910
of campaign 68 has been the strength of senator eugene mccarthy the volume with

01:28:05.910 --> 01:28:09.860
which new hampshire's voters today endorsed his effort signals trouble for

01:28:09.860 --> 01:28:14.100
president johnson's as yet undeclared reelection bid president and his

01:28:14.100 --> 01:28:18.680
advisers are most concerned about what tonight's returns mean in terms of bobby

01:28:18.680 --> 01:28:23.600
kennedy will gene mccarthy showing be enough to tempt kennedy into an open race

01:28:23.600 --> 01:28:27.770
for the democratic nomination we just don't know what bobby will do one of the

01:28:27.770 --> 01:28:29.430
president's closest friends said tonight

01:28:30.000 --> 01:28:32.580
and until we do know, we'll be wary.

01:28:32.860 --> 01:28:34.500
As I say, maybe I'll have something further to say

01:28:34.500 --> 01:28:36.320
after I see the rest of the figures.

01:28:36.420 --> 01:28:38.020
Would you accept the drafts out of it?

01:28:38.280 --> 01:28:39.720
I don't think anybody suggested that.

01:28:40.000 --> 01:28:41.900
Well, I'm suggesting it now. Would you accept it?

01:28:42.420 --> 01:28:44.060
I don't think that's a practical matter.

01:28:44.760 --> 01:28:45.580
Would you refuse it?

01:28:46.360 --> 01:28:47.940
Well, I just don't think you accept one.

01:28:50.580 --> 01:28:52.280
He retreats into a deep funk.

01:28:52.640 --> 01:28:54.380
He stops taking phone calls.

01:28:54.380 --> 01:28:55.800
He paces around his office.

01:28:55.860 --> 01:28:57.300
He won't talk to his aides.

01:28:57.840 --> 01:28:59.960
He goes back to Hickory Hill and seems to disappear.

01:29:00.060 --> 01:29:01.120
for several days.

01:29:02.020 --> 01:29:03.720
What he's doing during this time,

01:29:03.920 --> 01:29:06.480
besides castigating himself for making such a mistake,

01:29:06.780 --> 01:29:08.440
is deciding that he's going to run for president.

01:29:09.510 --> 01:29:11.570
And when he emerges, he comes out swinging.

01:29:12.060 --> 01:29:14.000
I am announcing today my candidacy

01:29:14.000 --> 01:29:17.250
for the presidency of the United States.

01:29:19.120 --> 01:29:22.200
I do not run for the presidency merely to oppose any man,

01:29:23.660 --> 01:29:25.180
but to propose new policy.

01:29:27.470 --> 01:29:29.970
Jackie Kennedy was much concerned, but...

01:29:30.320 --> 01:29:34.880
Robert Kennedy's entry into the presidential contest, she said,

01:29:35.190 --> 01:29:39.110
I believe they're going to do the same thing to him that they did to Jack.

01:29:55.980 --> 01:29:59.970
Kennedy's campaign in the state primaries was part politics, part...

01:30:00.000 --> 01:30:01.860
part crusade, part circus.

01:30:02.140 --> 01:30:07.910
In 15 days, he stormed through 16 states,

01:30:08.600 --> 01:30:11.220
tens of thousands of people screaming his name.

01:30:16.530 --> 01:30:19.530
My father was mobbed wherever he went.

01:30:19.940 --> 01:30:22.240
People were trying to touch him, trying to feel him,

01:30:22.440 --> 01:30:24.740
taking off his cufflinks, taking off his ties.

01:30:25.020 --> 01:30:26.750
I'd like to announce that somebody's

01:30:26.750 --> 01:30:28.190
taking off my shoe or something.

01:30:31.560 --> 01:30:39.310
Bob was at his best in that campaign, he touched people, he was speaking very directly to them,

01:30:39.430 --> 01:30:45.560
it was very heart to heart. He said he was doing it to save the soul of the country,

01:30:46.260 --> 01:30:51.940
and I believed him. We can return government to the people, we can change this nation around,

01:30:52.220 --> 01:30:58.340
we can make a new effort for peace in Vietnam, we can improve the life and the quality of

01:30:58.340 --> 01:30:59.960
America here in the United States.

01:31:00.000 --> 01:31:02.160
I ask for your help.

01:31:02.400 --> 01:31:04.200
Am I going to receive your help?

01:31:04.440 --> 01:31:09.700
When he first goes out on the stump, people go crazy.

01:31:10.070 --> 01:31:13.640
There's so much pent-up anger and frustration,

01:31:13.720 --> 01:31:16.670
and it's explosive when Bobby goes out there.

01:31:16.890 --> 01:31:22.020
He goes to the Midwest, you would think a bastion of conservatism,

01:31:22.580 --> 01:31:25.100
and he appears at the University of Kansas in Kansas State

01:31:25.100 --> 01:31:26.540
in these big field-up houses,

01:31:26.740 --> 01:31:28.690
and it's like the roof is blown off.

01:31:31.080 --> 01:31:41.090
When we get there, the place is just, not only is it packed, not only is it receipt packed,

01:31:41.720 --> 01:31:44.980
the entire floor is wall-to-wall people.

01:31:45.570 --> 01:31:49.670
And there are people sitting on the girders, practically hanging from the ceiling.

01:31:50.720 --> 01:31:54.440
And an unbelievable cacophony of noise.

01:31:58.000 --> 01:31:59.880
18,000 white.

01:32:00.220 --> 01:32:00.860
farm kids.

01:32:01.560 --> 01:32:03.440
in this big basketball field house.

01:32:04.600 --> 01:32:05.300
He gives.

01:32:05.750 --> 01:32:06.150
This.

01:32:06.900 --> 01:32:07.920
Powerful speech.

01:32:09.040 --> 01:32:11.860
against the war all out, finally, like it's...

01:32:12.310 --> 01:32:14.230
He's like a tiger let out of the cage.

01:32:15.000 --> 01:32:16.380
Our country is in danger.

01:32:17.110 --> 01:32:18.410
Not just from foreign enemies.

01:32:19.060 --> 01:32:21.620
but above all from our own misguided policies.

01:32:23.350 --> 01:32:24.790
This war must be ended.

01:32:25.720 --> 01:32:27.640
And in my judgment, it can be ended.

01:32:28.600 --> 01:32:30.000
And it does not involve giving up.

01:32:30.000 --> 01:32:38.270
But it does involve not continuing to follow the bankrupt policy that we're following at the present time.

01:32:41.110 --> 01:32:43.370
The stronger he gets, the louder the cheers.

01:32:44.420 --> 01:32:48.990
And I remember a photographer from Look Magazine looks over and he yells at me.

01:32:49.130 --> 01:32:51.690
He says, this is Kansas. This is Kansas.

01:33:00.980 --> 01:33:02.860
In spite of his wild popularity,

01:33:03.540 --> 01:33:06.000
Bobby knew he was fighting an uphill battle.

01:33:07.290 --> 01:33:09.770
The nomination was in the hands of the party bosses.

01:33:10.330 --> 01:33:12.530
Mayors, governors, labor leaders.

01:33:13.050 --> 01:33:15.530
who would control the votes at the Democratic Convention.

01:33:17.590 --> 01:33:20.190
I have to win through the people, Kennedy told a reporter.

01:33:20.750 --> 01:33:22.790
Otherwise, I'm not going to win.

01:33:24.150 --> 01:33:26.870
Then, on March 31, 1968,

01:33:27.480 --> 01:33:28.720
He was taken by surprise.

01:33:29.500 --> 01:33:29.940
Along

01:33:30.000 --> 01:33:31.100
with the rest of the nation.

01:33:32.010 --> 01:33:33.070
I shall not see.

01:33:35.370 --> 01:33:36.670
And I will not accept.

01:33:38.090 --> 01:33:41.570
the nomination of my party for another term as your president.

01:33:43.750 --> 01:33:44.630
with no warning.

01:33:44.940 --> 01:33:47.540
a physically and emotionally exhausted president.

01:33:48.140 --> 01:33:50.720
had put an end to the contest with Robert Kennedy.

01:33:51.180 --> 01:33:53.460
just 15 days after it had begun.

01:33:55.020 --> 01:33:56.360
I wonder, Bobby said,

01:33:57.000 --> 01:33:58.160
if he would have done this.

01:33:58.570 --> 01:33:59.890
if I hadn't come in.

01:34:04.520 --> 01:34:07.500
Four days later, Bobby flew to Indianapolis

01:34:07.500 --> 01:34:09.900
for a campaign speech in a black neighborhood.

01:34:10.880 --> 01:34:13.200
En route, a reporter had told him

01:34:13.200 --> 01:34:15.600
that Martin Luther King had been murdered.

01:34:15.820 --> 01:34:25.160
We were trying to pull together people for a mass rally

01:34:25.160 --> 01:34:27.440
for Bobby Kennedy, but there were some people

01:34:27.440 --> 01:34:29.960
saying that evening that maybe he shouldn't come.

01:34:32.240 --> 01:34:34.030
because maybe that would be violence.

01:34:36.030 --> 01:34:37.330
The police thought it was dangerous.

01:34:37.690 --> 01:34:40.610
They didn't want us to go in there, but he went.

01:34:40.770 --> 01:34:46.900
I scribbled something on a piece of paper

01:34:46.900 --> 01:34:48.280
because I knew he'd want to say something,

01:34:48.890 --> 01:34:51.410
but he had figured out what he was going to say.

01:34:51.510 --> 01:34:52.350
He had written it himself.

01:34:54.410 --> 01:34:55.410
We brought them the news

01:34:55.410 --> 01:34:57.400
that Martin Luther King had been shot.

01:35:00.090 --> 01:35:02.190
Very sad news for all of you.

01:35:03.000 --> 01:35:04.600
And I think, uh, sad news.

01:35:05.370 --> 01:35:06.630
for all of our fellow citizens.

01:35:08.020 --> 01:35:09.020
And people who love.

01:35:09.370 --> 01:35:10.730
Peace all over the world.

01:35:11.930 --> 01:35:12.710
And that is it.

01:35:13.370 --> 01:35:16.070
Martin Luther King was shot and was killed tonight.

01:35:19.990 --> 01:35:20.950
Most of the people hadn't.

01:35:21.080 --> 01:35:22.740
Hadn't even heard that Dr. K had been shot.

01:35:23.000 --> 01:35:23.740
And we will start.

01:35:24.980 --> 01:35:25.900
And we all cried.

01:35:27.990 --> 01:35:29.930
But that evening, Robert Kennedy spoke for me.

01:35:30.000 --> 01:35:30.320
soul.

01:35:31.140 --> 01:35:32.620
For those of you who are...

01:35:33.640 --> 01:35:34.100
Black.

01:35:35.370 --> 01:35:36.710
and are attempted to fail with

01:35:37.000 --> 01:35:37.660
be filled with

01:35:38.020 --> 01:35:38.500
Hatred in.

01:35:39.080 --> 01:35:39.680
distrust

01:35:41.700 --> 01:35:43.260
of the injustice of such an act.

01:35:45.700 --> 01:35:46.940
Against all white people?

01:35:48.680 --> 01:35:50.280
I would only say that I can also...

01:35:50.920 --> 01:35:52.040
Feel in my own heart.

01:35:53.100 --> 01:35:54.060
same kind of feeling.

01:35:55.750 --> 01:35:57.310
I had a member of my family killed.

01:35:59.080 --> 01:35:59.820
But he was killed.

01:36:00.120 --> 01:36:06.780
white man. But we have to make an effort in the United States. We have to make an

01:36:06.780 --> 01:36:11.670
effort to understand. We go beyond these rather difficult times.

01:36:12.500 --> 01:36:23.590
The words, they just ring. They'll just chill your body. And he did it not in a loud but

01:36:24.120 --> 01:36:26.220
almost in a prayerful manner.

01:36:26.990 --> 01:36:29.550
My favorite poet was Aeschylus.

01:36:30.510 --> 01:36:43.340
He once wrote, even in our sleep, pain which cannot forget falls drop by drop upon the heart

01:36:43.340 --> 01:36:53.090
until in our own day despair against our will comes wisdom through the awful grace of God.

01:36:56.120 --> 01:36:58.340
In our sleep, pain that cannot forget.

01:37:00.960 --> 01:37:02.980
falls drop by drop upon the heart

01:37:04.890 --> 01:37:07.230
until in our despair against our will

01:37:08.600 --> 01:37:11.370
comes wisdom through the awful grace of God.

01:37:14.160 --> 01:37:17.560
There were riots in more than 100 cities across America

01:37:17.560 --> 01:37:18.820
after King's death.

01:37:20.210 --> 01:37:23.700
But that night, there was calm in Indianapolis.

01:37:36.230 --> 01:37:40.070
There was no way that Eugene McCarthy could inspire crowds

01:37:40.070 --> 01:37:43.480
the way Bobby Kennedy did, but he didn't seem to care.

01:37:45.900 --> 01:37:50.420
Cool and aloof, he appealed to white middle-class opponents of the war,

01:37:50.780 --> 01:37:52.040
especially college students,

01:37:52.300 --> 01:37:55.260
scornful of Kennedy's late entry into the race.

01:37:58.510 --> 01:37:59.970
Bobby Kennedy made a natural...

01:38:00.000 --> 01:38:06.860
and it pained him deeply when the best students often signed up with Gene McCarthy.

01:38:07.500 --> 01:38:10.460
Robert Kennedy loved the McCarthy kids.

01:38:11.040 --> 01:38:16.860
He once said to me, it drives me crazy that Gene gets all the A students and I get all the C students.

01:38:20.410 --> 01:38:24.490
On election night, Indiana voters gave Bobby the victory he wanted,

01:38:25.080 --> 01:38:27.100
but not the knockout blow he needed.

01:38:28.100 --> 01:38:29.920
This was his first face-to-face.

01:38:30.000 --> 01:38:36.720
primary with McCarthy. He had a winner and he did win it. But winning Indiana was just the first

01:38:36.720 --> 01:38:44.020
inning of a nine-inning game. A week later, Bobby proved that he could appeal to farmers,

01:38:44.420 --> 01:38:53.210
winning again, this time in Nebraska. Oregon was next. The intention of the nation is going to

01:38:53.210 --> 01:38:57.270
be focused on the state of Oregon. You could very well determine who's going to be the next

01:38:57.270 --> 01:38:59.980
president of the United States. But Oregon...

01:39:00.000 --> 01:39:03.180
were mostly comfortable white suburban voters.

01:39:04.120 --> 01:39:06.900
They turned away from Bobby's campaign

01:39:06.900 --> 01:39:09.040
with its focus on race and poverty.

01:39:09.450 --> 01:39:16.580
For the first time ever, a Kennedy lost an election.

01:39:18.420 --> 01:39:20.600
Let's face it, Bobby told a reporter,

01:39:21.220 --> 01:39:23.620
I appeal best to people who have problems.

01:39:24.760 --> 01:39:26.740
He was devastated by that loss.

01:39:27.310 --> 01:39:29.790
He said if only we could have moved a ghetto up here for a day.

01:39:30.000 --> 01:39:38.020
could have won this election. It was not going to be a victory march that he had hoped for.

01:39:38.450 --> 01:39:59.420
He had to win California. He just absolutely had to win. California was pure mayhem. People treated him

01:40:00.000 --> 01:40:09.080
he was some rock star. It was young people, it was blacks, white, Hispanic, just pulling for him.

01:40:10.470 --> 01:40:18.280
It was the most emotional adulation I've ever seen in politics, particularly in the black and

01:40:18.280 --> 01:40:27.460
Mexican areas. The Sunday before the primary, he invited me to ride in his car going through Watts

01:40:27.460 --> 01:40:28.840
and he's still there.

01:40:30.000 --> 01:40:42.200
He said to me, I want you to see what I see and I see this ecstasy in the eyes of Blacks and Mexican Americans.

01:40:42.500 --> 01:40:59.880
If we have that love and friendship and understanding for our fellow citizens, we will have a new America.

01:41:00.380 --> 01:41:08.070
and you will have made it possible, and I will work with all of you.

01:41:08.250 --> 01:41:09.490
Give me your help!

01:41:12.370 --> 01:41:17.670
Not since Abraham Lincoln had a white politician been so embraced by people of color.

01:41:19.160 --> 01:41:21.380
These are my people, he told an aide.

01:41:21.740 --> 01:41:23.060
These are my people.

01:41:28.060 --> 01:41:29.640
On the final day of the campaign,

01:41:30.320 --> 01:41:33.920
Bobby and Ethel rode slowly through San Francisco's Chinatown.

01:41:35.350 --> 01:41:39.380
As usual, there were no armed bodyguards or secret service agents.

01:41:40.940 --> 01:41:44.740
You've just got to give yourself to the people and to trust them, Bobby said.

01:41:45.260 --> 01:41:49.280
And from then on, either luck is with you or it isn't.

01:41:58.820 --> 01:41:59.980
What sounded like shock?

01:42:00.000 --> 01:42:02.700
turned out to be Chinese firecrackers.

01:42:03.650 --> 01:42:06.810
Bobby flinched, then went right on campaigning.

01:42:07.860 --> 01:42:11.360
Robert Kennedy was fearless to the cusp of reckless,

01:42:11.790 --> 01:42:15.480
and I think there was some daring of death and fate

01:42:16.310 --> 01:42:17.650
in what he did.

01:42:25.050 --> 01:42:27.820
On election night, there was only good news.

01:42:29.070 --> 01:42:29.970
Bobby got the victory.

01:42:30.000 --> 01:42:34.660
he needed with blacks and Hispanics voting for him in overwhelming numbers.

01:42:35.680 --> 01:42:41.350
The 1,500 volunteers jamming the ballroom at the Ambassador Hotel in Los Angeles

01:42:41.350 --> 01:42:44.510
were ecstatic as they waited for Bobby to appear.

01:42:45.070 --> 01:42:54.460
It's a lot of people there, a lot of people. Everybody, you know, really having a

01:42:54.460 --> 01:42:58.700
wonderful time and getting ready for a terrific party that was going to take

01:42:58.700 --> 01:42:59.260
place later.

01:43:03.160 --> 01:43:04.440
This was really his win.

01:43:04.840 --> 01:43:06.140
Hadn't been done by staff,

01:43:06.300 --> 01:43:08.080
hadn't been done by all these people around him.

01:43:08.320 --> 01:43:08.920
He did it.

01:43:09.480 --> 01:43:13.410
He had liberated himself from leaning on his brother's myth.

01:43:14.400 --> 01:43:17.050
He had found that inner voice.

01:43:18.940 --> 01:43:21.760
Feel now for the first time, Bobby told an aide,

01:43:22.410 --> 01:43:24.900
that I've shaken off the shadow of my brother.

01:43:32.750 --> 01:43:36.830
We watched him speaking from the ballroom of the Ambassador Hotel,

01:43:37.280 --> 01:43:39.720
and we were always feeling very, very good.

01:43:40.030 --> 01:43:45.200
What I think is quite clear is that we can work together in the last analysis,

01:43:45.510 --> 01:43:47.970
and that what has been going on within the United States

01:43:47.970 --> 01:43:49.750
over the period of the last three years,

01:43:50.300 --> 01:43:54.830
the divisions, the violence, the disenchantment with our society,

01:43:55.190 --> 01:43:57.190
the divisions, whether it's between blacks and whites,

01:43:57.350 --> 01:43:59.980
between the poor and the more affluent, or between a...

01:44:00.000 --> 01:44:04.440
groups are on the war in vietnam that we can start to work together we are a great country

01:44:04.440 --> 01:44:09.980
and a selfish country and a compassionate country and i intend to make that my basis for running

01:44:09.980 --> 01:44:20.600
and over the period of my time so my thanks to all of you and now it's on to chicago and let's win

01:44:40.240 --> 01:44:46.230
I left the suite on the fifth floor during RFK's acceptance speech,

01:44:47.440 --> 01:44:51.740
got into the ballroom just as he was leaving the podium,

01:44:52.620 --> 01:44:59.720
and he goes into the kitchen area, and I hear this moan.

01:45:00.030 --> 01:45:01.970
This cry, this screams.

01:45:11.360 --> 01:45:15.820
As Bobby had reached out to shake the hands of the men and women who worked in the kitchen,

01:45:16.560 --> 01:45:21.360
24-year-old Saran Saran fired a bullet into his brain.

01:45:31.530 --> 01:45:35.250
Well, delirium broke loose, and I realized that he had been shot.

01:45:37.660 --> 01:45:49.110
People just went nuts, so people punched the walls, lie on the ground and weep.

01:45:50.460 --> 01:45:53.280
John Lewis and I just hugged each other and sobbed.

01:45:54.240 --> 01:45:59.180
Dr. King, two months earlier, and now Robert Kennedy, but just too much.

01:46:03.870 --> 01:46:07.860
On June 6th, 1968, Bobby died.

01:46:12.160 --> 01:46:14.680
Like his brother less than five years before,

01:46:15.840 --> 01:46:18.910
Robert Kennedy passed on into legend.

01:46:28.180 --> 01:46:29.980
Two days after Bobby's death...

01:46:30.990 --> 01:46:36.940
His body was carried to Washington's Arlington Cemetery, accompanied by family and friends.

01:46:37.200 --> 01:46:53.140
It was like an Irish wake. Everybody was in pain. Everybody was numb, in shock.

01:46:55.040 --> 01:46:59.970
Just one long cortege.

01:47:00.190 --> 01:47:02.370
hour after hour after hour.

01:47:02.530 --> 01:47:15.190
All along the way, you saw these unbelievable crowds

01:47:15.190 --> 01:47:16.390
carrying signs saying,

01:47:16.510 --> 01:47:18.090
we love you Bobby, goodbye Bobby.

01:47:18.290 --> 01:47:29.970
You saw white faces and black...

01:47:30.000 --> 01:47:36.280
faces and Latino and all the diversity of America it was all there on the side

01:47:36.280 --> 01:47:48.360
of the tracks as the train went back to Washington it was quite remarkable

01:47:48.360 --> 01:47:53.860
really what Bobby Kennedy suddenly meant to the American people and I say

01:47:53.860 --> 01:47:59.140
suddenly because it had happened in just a couple of years really I mean it would

01:47:59.140 --> 01:47:59.880
have been laughable

01:48:00.000 --> 01:48:01.100
to think of Bobby Kennedy.

01:48:02.040 --> 01:48:04.020
presidential candidate in 1961.

01:48:05.240 --> 01:48:07.440
and it certainly wasn't laughable in 1968.

01:48:14.330 --> 01:48:15.270
He had the capacity.

01:48:16.210 --> 01:48:17.170
to open up himself.

01:48:18.810 --> 01:48:19.730
We saw him grow.

01:48:20.980 --> 01:48:22.040
We saw him change.

01:48:30.090 --> 01:48:31.470
He was 42 when he was killed.

01:48:33.830 --> 01:48:36.490
We'll never know would he really have gotten us out of Vietnam.

01:48:37.510 --> 01:48:39.750
when he really dealt with poverty and racism.

01:48:41.830 --> 01:48:44.110
But he was cheated out of his chance to...

01:48:44.580 --> 01:48:46.720
to test his ideas and his values.

01:48:50.310 --> 01:48:54.870
It seems to me that what we're doing when we warn Robert Kennedy...

01:48:55.850 --> 01:48:56.750
is mourning

01:49:00.380 --> 01:49:01.900
our own lost possibilities.

01:49:16.760 --> 01:49:18.720
30 yards from his brother's grave.

01:49:19.700 --> 01:49:21.500
Robert Kennedy was laid to rest.

01:49:30.600 --> 01:49:32.320
carved on the marble gravestone.

01:49:32.780 --> 01:49:35.880
are the words from Aeschylus that he could recite from memory.

01:49:37.060 --> 01:49:38.040
Thank you.

01:49:38.470 --> 01:49:40.550
He who learns must suffer.

01:49:41.900 --> 01:49:43.100
and even in our sleep.

01:49:43.780 --> 01:49:45.260
pain that cannot forget.

01:49:46.180 --> 01:49:48.620
falls drop by drop upon the heart.

01:49:49.860 --> 01:49:51.220
And in our own despair,

01:49:52.330 --> 01:49:53.530
against our will.

01:49:54.950 --> 01:49:56.310
comes wisdom to us.

01:49:57.380 --> 01:49:58.720
by the awful grace.

01:49:59.750 --> 01:49:59.950
gone.

